TY  - JOUR
AB  - Accurate monitoring of training load is vital for optimizing performance and reducing the risk of maladaptation, injury, or illness in cyclists. Training load quantification also informs nutrition planning, as daily energy and macronutrient requirements are often adjusted based on exercise demands. Although a range of metrics is available, from power output-based measures to heart rate and perceived exertion indicators, students and early-career practitioners often encounter difficulties in selecting, calculating, and interpreting these indices. This methodology review outlines how to compute three commonly used training load metrics in cycling: training stress score, Edwards' training impulse, and session rating of perceived exertion. We provide step-by-step guidance on evaluating and predicting these metrics using practical, well-illustrated examples drawn from the data of a competitive cyclist. The methodology review includes the visualization and interpretation of relationships between metrics via scatterplots and regression analyses, both with and without intercepts, and in linear and curvilinear forms. We also introduce the use of partial correlation to examine associations between metrics while controlling for exercise duration thereby addressing a frequent confounding factor. A worked example illustrates how data can be used to derive session-specific carbohydrate and energy targets. By following the procedures outlined here, undergraduate and postgraduate students as well as early-career practitioners can enhance their understanding of training load monitoring and develop tailored strategies suited to individual cyclists and contextual constraints including training and nutrition periodization decisions. The included spreadsheet instructions and supplementary R script support replication and facilitate the integration of these methods into applied practice. This paper is a practical guide to understanding how hard a cyclist has trained and how that information can be used in day-to-day coaching and nutrition planning. The authors argue that keeping track of training demands matters because it can help improve performance; lower the risk of poor recovery, illness, or injury; and give better estimates of how much food, especially carbohydrate, an athlete may need before, during, and after different sessions. Rather than testing a new theory, the paper explains, step by step, how to calculate and compare three common ways of measuring training load in cycling. One method is based on power output, one is based on heart rate, and one is based on the rider's own rating of how hard the whole session felt. The main goal is educational: to help students and newer practitioners understand not just how to produce these numbers but how to judge what they do and do not mean. The first method uses power data from a bicycle power meter. The paper explains that average power alone can be misleading because two rides can have the same average but place very different demands on the body. To deal with that, the method uses a weighted form of power that gives more importance to harder efforts and then combines this with the rider's best sustainable effort level to create a training load score. The second method uses heart rate. It adds up the time spent in different heart rate ranges, giving more weight to time spent at higher intensities. The third method is the simplest: After the ride, the cyclist gives one overall rating for how hard the session felt, and that number is multiplied by how long the session lasted. The paper makes clear that each approach has practical advantages and drawbacks. Power based methods can be very informative but require equipment that may be costly or unavailable. Heart rate is more widely available but can be affected by heat, stress, and hydration. Self-reported effort is cheap and easy to use, but it is subjective and can also be influenced by how long the ride lasted. To show how these methods work in practice, the authors use the training records of one experienced competitive male cyclist collected over 23 weeks, covering 76 sessions. They then show how to place the different training load measures side by side and compare them using simple graphs and equations. A key message is that these comparisons should be done for each athlete individually because the relationships between measures can differ from person to person and from one type of training to another. The paper also warns readers not to trust software outputs blindly. In particular, it points out that some chart settings can produce misleading goodness-of-fit values, especially when using curved lines forced to pass through zero. The authors therefore encourage careful checking and favor simpler models when they are just as useful. One of the most important parts of the paper is its attempt to separate true agreement between measures from the simple fact that longer sessions usually produce bigger numbers in every system. To do that, the authors remove the effect of session length and then examine how closely the measures still match. In this cyclist, the self-reported effort score and the power-based score stayed reasonably well aligned after session length was taken into account. By contrast, the heart rate-based score matched the other two less closely. The practical meaning is that, for this rider, self-reported effort may be a useful substitute when power data are missing, while the heart rate-based method may be capturing something a bit different and should be used more cautiously for prediction. The paper then takes an extra step by showing how training load data can help estimate how much carbohydrate a cyclist used and how much energy the session likely costed. In other words, the training load metrics are not only useful for judging how hard someone trained but also for helping decide how much fuel they may need. This part is especially relevant for sports nutrition because it supports the idea of adjusting food intake to the actual work completed rather than using the same eating plan every day. Overall, the paper's central message is that no single training load measure is perfect or universally best. Each one reflects a different side of the training experience, and the most useful approach is to understand their strengths, weaknesses, and how they relate within each individual athlete. The authors present these tools as practical estimates rather than exact measures of what is happening inside the body. Their broader contribution is to offer a clear, reproducible framework that can help coaches, students, and practitioners make better training and nutrition decisions while also remembering that findings from one rider should not be assumed to apply to everyone else. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
