AB  - Acute Physiological and Locomotor Responses to Fixed-Area Transition Small-Sided Games of Different Pitch Sizes and Work-to-Rest Ratios in Soccer Players. The purpose of this study was to describe the internal- and external-load responses of a fixed-area (no pitch-area change) transition small-sided game (SSG) format in which the players transitioned from defense to attack without altering the playing area across 3 pitch sizes (small, 75 m²·player⁻¹; medium, 100 m²·player⁻¹; large, 150 m²·player⁻¹) and 2 work-to-rest ratios (1:1, 4 min:4 min; 1:0.5, 4 min:2 min). Twenty-two male soccer players (N = 22) volunteered, forming 6 fixed 3-a-side teams that completed all 6 pitch-size × ratio conditions of the fixed-area format, supported in every trial by 4 floating attacking-support players who preserved the defense-to-attack transition structure. Heart rate (HR), rating of perceived exertion (RPE, Borg CR-10), and 10-Hz GPS-derived locomotor variables were recorded during each bout and analyzed with Jamovi. Across the 6 conditions, mean HR ranged from 151.0 ± 7.7 to 164.3 ± 9.6 beats·min⁻¹ (75.2 - 82.3% of HRmax), RPE ranged from 5.7 ± 0.9 to 7.8 ± 0.9, and total distance covered ranged from 1508.6 ± 212.6 to 2185.0 ± 265.2 m. The medium pitch produced the highest relative cardiovascular strain (%HRmean, RPE) at both ratios; whereas, the total distance, high-speed running, and the number of accelerations/decelerations increased with pitch size and were consistently lowest on the small pitch. Shortening the recovery interval (1:0.5) increased RPE on the medium and large pitches but not on the small pitch. These findings indicate that, within a fixed-area transition SSG format, pitch size and work-to-rest ratio act on internal- and external-load markers in a non-uniform, partially dissociated manner, giving coaches 2 independent levers for prescribing the aerobictechnical training stimulus. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
