AB  - Purpose: To compare effects of block periodization against evenly distributed low-intensity training on endurance performance in well-trained junior cross-country (XC) skiers. Methods: Twenty junior cross-country skiers participated in a crossover design study. Athletes were matched by maximal oxygen uptake (VO<sub>2</sub>max) and sex and were randomly assigned to either BLOCK or EVEN interventions, each lasting 4 weeks, separated by a 1-week break. BLOCK performed 1 week at 160% of baseline endurance volume, followed by weeks of reduced volume, while EVEN maintained consistent volume. Total training volume was identical between conditions. Pretesting and posttesting measured VO<sub>2</sub>max, maximal aerobic speed (V<sub>max</sub>), and submaximal performance indices. Subjective markers of stress and recovery were also assessed daily. Results: No significant treatment–time interaction effects were found between the BLOCK and EVEN interventions for any performance variable, including VO<sub>2</sub>max (P =.83, d = −0.1), V<sub>max</sub> (P =.15, d = −0.4), or submaximal speed at 3 mmol⋅L<sup>−1</sup> blood lactate concentration (V<sub>3mmol/L</sub>) (P =.55, d = 0.2). While both groups demonstrated significant within-group improvements in some performance variables, these gains were not statistically different between the 2 periodization strategies. The BLOCK group, however, reported significantly lower mood and perceived recovery during the high-volume week and the subsequent recovery week. Conclusion: Block periodization of low-intensity training is not more effective for improving endurance performance in junior cross-country skiers than volume-matched, evenly distributed training. The concentrated, high-volume week of low-intensity training was well-tolerated and safe but failed to provide an additional performance stimulus. The findings indicate that for training with a lower physiological stress profile, consistent application, rather than concentration, is the primary driver of adaptation. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
