AB  - This study investigated pre-competition sleep quality and its relationship with recovery-stress indices and performance indicators in competitive cyclists. Twenty competitive cyclists (age: 20.8 ± 2.0 years) completed assessments of body composition, resting heart rate (RHR), standing long jump (SLJ), the Acute Recovery and Stress Scale (ARSS), and the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI). Participants were stratified into good sleepers (PSQI =5) and poor sleepers (PSQI >5). The mean global PSQI score (7.7 ± 1.8) indicated predominantly poor precompetition sleep. Spearman's correlations revealed moderate associations between global PSQI and Recovery Index (ρ = -0.41), Stress Index (ρ = 0.48), and Recovery-Stress Balance (ρ = -0.52; all p < 0.05). Good sleepers showed superior Recovery-Stress Balance (2.50 ± 1.68 vs. 1.71 ± 0.93), lower Stress Index (1.72 ± 0.13 vs. 2.48 ± 0.59), and greater SLJ performance (265.0 ± 7.1 vs. 231.4 ± 38.1 cm) compared to poor sleepers. Although between-group differences did not reach statistical significance, large effect sizes were observed for stress index and standing long jump performance, suggesting potentially meaningful differences despite the limited sample size. Routine monitoring of sleep and recovery-stress status through validated questionnaires helps in identifying athletes with less favourable pre-competition profiles and provides practical support for individualised training load management and sleep hygiene strategies. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
