AB  - The purpose of this literature review was to explore the impact of concurrent strength and endurance training on long-distance triathlon (LDT) performance, addressing its benefits, potential barriers, and practical applications. Long-distance triathletes must meet the complex physiological demands of swimming, cycling, and running, with performance largely determined by aerobic capacity, muscular endurance, and energy efficiency. Strength training (ST) has been shown to improve key performance metrics, such as cycling power and running economy, while mitigating fatigue. Despite this, only 55-63% of LDT athletes incorporate ST into their routines. A search of two databases (PubMed and Google Scholar) found 19 studies eligible for review. The review identified that ST, particularly heavy resistance and plyometric exercises, enhances neuromuscular coordination, musculotendinous stiffness, and energy economy. These adaptations improve endurance performance by reducing submaximal energy expenditure and delaying fatigue. Despite the benefits, the interference effect between endurance and strength training adaptations remains a significant concern. Evidence indicates that structuring programs and tailoring training to individual needs can mitigate this effect. Barriers to ST adoption among long distance triathletes can be addressed through targeted education for athletes and coaches on the benefits and implementation of concurrent training. This review concludes that concurrent training may improve strength and endurance qualities in LDT athletes whilst mitigating injury risk. Long-distance triathletes should incorporate 2-3 strength sessions weekly (2-3 hours), focusing on heavy resistance (e.g., squats, deadlifts, lunges) and plyometrics (e.g., box jumps, bounding, pogo hops) whilst ensuring the sessions are periodised and tailored to optimise adaption by minimising endurance training interference. Further research is needed to refine protocols and address individual variability in concurrent training responses specific to long distance triathlon performance. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
