AB  - It is recommended that endurance athletes modulate their daily carbohydrate intake according to the demands of training, but there is limited evidence of how this is currently practiced by athletes during real-world, day-to-day training. The purpose of this observational study was to report the dietary intake of endurance athletes across a 12-week period with an emphasis on the relationship between training load and carbohydrate intake. Self-selected training and dietary intake were self-reported using a smartphone app by 46 endurance athletes (61% male) daily for 12 weeks, representing a total of 3,718 days of dietary assessments and 3,160 days of training. Fasted-state training was regularly performed by 65% of athletes and was more common in men (33.6% vs. 17.2% of training days, p =.023). Average daily carbohydrate intake for each athlete ranged from 1.2 to 7.2 g/kg (M = 3.9 ± 1.5). At the group level, significant correlations were found between mean daily carbohydrate intake and both percentage of training sessions performed in the fasted state (r = −.39, p =.008) and weekly training volume (r =.42, p =.004). Participant-level correlations between daily training load and carbohydrate intake ranged from −.42 to.83. Overall, athletes adjust daily carbohydrate intake based on exercise duration, but at the individual level, many athletes do not align carbohydrate intake with training load as recommended or do so with minimal adjustment. Endurance athletes are often told to "fuel for the work required," meaning adjust their daily carbohydrate intake to match how hard and how long they train. This idea makes intuitive sense: Harder, longer workouts burn more carbohydrate and call for more refueling, whereas easier or rest days should generally require less. In practice, however, there has yet to be a clear way to measure how well athletes follow this guidance. Most of what we know has come from short surveys or case studies that do not fully capture real-life day-to-day eating habits. This study tracked the training and daily food intake of 55 endurance athletes over 12 weeks—collecting ∼4,000 days of food logs and training information. This allowed us to see, on a daily basis, whether and how much each athlete adjusted their carbohydrate intake in response to training demands. At the group level, athletes generally ate more carbohydrate on days when they trained longer, suggesting some alignment with the "fuel for the work required" concept. However, when looking at individuals, the picture was more varied. Some athletes showed a strong link between training load and carbohydrate intake, whereas others showed little or no adjustment at all. Many athletes appeared to change their intake only slightly, even on very hard or very easy training days. We also found that fasted training was common, with nearly two thirds of athletes doing this at least once per week. Athletes who consumed less carbohydrate overall tended to perform more fasted training, but fasted training was not related to how much total training athletes completed each week. An important secondary finding was that short periods of dietary tracking may not accurately represent an athlete's typical intake. For many athletes, several weeks of food logging were needed before average energy and macronutrient intake could be estimated with reasonable confidence. Overall, these findings suggest that although endurance athletes broadly understand the idea of adjusting carbohydrate intake to training, many do not apply it consistently or to a meaningful degree in practice. The results highlight large individual differences in fueling behavior and reinforce the importance of personalized nutrition guidance as well as longer term dietary monitoring when evaluating how athletes fuel their training. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
