TY  - JOUR
AB  - Bicycle motorcross racing is a unique sprint cycling discipline that takes place on a 300- to 450-m outdoor track with an 8-m start hill. A single race is 30–40 s in duration, and athletes can compete in up to seven races per day. Athletes require highly specific physiological attributes to meet the training and competition demands, and successful riders can produce high peak power outputs and demonstrate technical and tactical superiority over their opponents. Nutrition has a key role in optimizing athlete health, training adaptations, and overall athlete performance. This review will focus on the training and race demands for bicycle motorcross race and fueling strategies to support training and racing outcomes. Bicycle motorcross (BMX) racing is a short, high-speed cycling discipline in which riders compete over 30–40 s on a track consisting of jumps and turns. What seems like a brief effort is actually part of a demanding day during which top riders may race up to seven times. These repeated sprints are physically demanding, especially when competing or training in hot weather. Top BMX athletes are powerful and muscular, capable of generating high levels of lower body power output while using their upper body to control the bike over jumps and through turns. Success is largely determined by how fast and explosively a rider can launch from the start gate and gain position into the first corner, usually within the first 8–10 s of the race. To meet the sport's physical demands, riders rely mostly on fast-burning energy systems in the body, especially during explosive starts. Although the races are short, energy from food, particularly carbohydrates, is still vital across a long race day to support performance and decision-making ability. Training includes multiple weekly sessions on the track and in the gym and performing sprints. Gym work can make up 30%–50% of total training time, focusing on strength and power. Track sessions often exceed 2 hr and target technical skills like cornering, jumping, and gate starts. Sprint sessions develop the power and pedaling speed needed for racing. To meet these demands, nutrition plays a critical role. Riders need the following: • Protein: 1.6–2.2 g/kg of body mass per day to support muscle growth and recovery, with 0.3–0.4 g/kg per meal spread across the day. A slow-digesting casein protein (∼30–60 g) before bed can also aid overnight recovery. • Carbohydrates: 4–10 g/kg per day to fuel training and racing. Riders typically consume 1–2 g/kg before training and adjust their intake around gym and track work. Carbohydrates before strength training can enhance output and reduce fatigue. • Hydration: Maintaining hydration is essential. Dehydration of more than 2% of body weight can impair both physical performance and skill. Cool fluids, electrolyte drinks, and ice slurries help in hot conditions. During racing, riders may consume over 1.5 L of fluids across warm-ups and heats. Riders face logistical and environmental challenges, including frequent international travel and extreme weather. At events, cooling strategies including ice towels, shaded areas, and cold fluids are used to keep core temperatures down. Heat stress is a real risk, and simple measures like drinking icy drinks between heats may aid physical performance and decision making. Although BMX entered the Olympics in 2008, research on how to fuel for BMX is still limited. This review pulls together what is currently known and identifies clear opportunities for future research to develop tailored nutrition and recovery strategies specific to BMX race athletes. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
