TY  - JOUR
AB  - Track cycling is a unique discipline whereby events take place on a velodrome using fixed-gear bicycles. Events cover a spectrum of durations ranging from <11 s through to ∼60 min. Therefore, diverse and specialized physiological attributes are required to meet the specific demands of competition. Nutrition has a fundamental role in optimizing athlete performance through maintaining overall health, fueling training to develop the required physiological characteristics for success and enabling athletes to meet the energy demands of competition. This review will focus on how nutrition can be optimized to best support the training periodization and competition requirements and provide practical recommendations on fueling strategies for track cycling. Track cycling is a unique form of cycling, whereby events take place on a velodrome (usually a wooden indoor 250-m track) using a fixed-gear bicycle. Track cycling consists of events, which range from 9 s in duration up to 55 min, and the events are completed solo, in a team, or as a mass start. Males and females compete in separate races, and athletes compete in either a sprint or endurance racing. Sprint cyclists compete in the shorter races (e.g., 9 s) and are more powerful than endurance cyclists. For athletes to achieve training adaptations and success at international competition, athletes require an individualized training and nutrition plan in combination with input on technical, tactical, physical, and psychological elements of the sport. This review outlines the specific elements of sprint and endurance cycling and how nutrition can support training and racing, focusing on Olympic events. Nutrition has a fundamental role in supporting athlete health, training, and competition goals; however, quantifying the volume of food an athlete is required to consume to meet these goals can be difficult, particularly in sprint cycling where efforts are short and above maximal in nature. In endurance cycling, technology such as power meters and heart rate monitors are available tools and commonly used in supporting the estimation of energy requirements of cycling. Athletes often race multiple times per day, and schedules change depending on the race, for example, Olympic Games, World Championships, and Nations Cup all have different schedules. Race recovery can range from 1 hr to more than 24 hr. It is therefore important for an athletes' warm-up, warm-downs, and food intake to be carefully planned around the race day schedule for both endurance and sprint. For track endurance athletes, warm-ups can last 20–35 min; whereas, for sprint athletes, this is typically 15–25 min. Warm-downs for endurance and sprint cyclists typically involve riding at a low intensity for 5–20 min between events. Endurance events rely on carbohydrate-based fuels as the dominant fuel source for racing, and with finite stores, athletes are required to increase carbohydrate intake in the days leading into an event. During racing, a unique aspect of track cycling is the inability to consume any food or fluid during races, unlike other forms of cycling (e.g., road cycling) where this is common practice. On race day morning, athletes often eat an easily digested, low fiber meal, aligned to athlete preference, to minimize risk of gut distress around high-intensity racing. It is also recommended that athletes consume carbohydrate and protein immediately after racing and consume protein prior to sleep to promote recovery, in preparation for racing on consecutive days. Sprint athletes are also recommended to consume carbohydrate throughout race days and a combination of carbohydrate and protein for recovery. While the form of energy may be similar in sprint and endurance, it is essential that the volume of intake aligns with the individual athlete requirement and food preferences align with athlete preference. Limited research is published on nutrition for track sprint and endurance cycling, and further research is required to understand the current nutrition practice of elite track cyclists to provide more specific future recommendations. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
