TY  - JOUR
AB  - Elite/professional cyclists often experience injuries and illnesses that lead to loss of training time/quality and reduced performance. Consequently, mitigating these problems is a high priority for multiple stakeholders, including athletes, coaches, sports medicine and allied health practitioners, and race organizers. This Union Cycliste Internationale-endorsed paper reviews the evidence for nutritional interventions in preventing and managing common injuries and illnesses in elite cycling, including skin injuries, upper respiratory tract infections, gastrointestinal disturbances, and sports-related concussions. Ensuring adequate protein (1.5–2.0 g·kg<sup>−1</sup>·day<sup>−1</sup>) and consumption of key micronutrients involved in wound healing (e.g., vitamin C and zinc) may optimize skin healing, albeit with no direct evidence in cyclists. Nutritional management strategies for upper respiratory tract infection include ingesting appropriate amounts of carbohydrate and protein to support training loads, optimizing vitamin D status, and possibly probiotic and polyphenol supplementation. Supplementation with other nutrients (omega-3 fats, glutamine, and vitamin C) also has come with some supportive, albeit mixed, evidence. Short-term low fermentable oligosaccharides, disaccharides, monosaccharides, and polyols diets; gut training; and use of mixed saccharide (glucose/maltodextrin–fructose) foods/supplements are evidence-supported strategies for reducing gastrointestinal symptoms, while probiotic supplementation, carbohydrate hydrogels, and cool/cold beverages currently have equivocal evidence. Promoting personal hygiene and food safety principles are important factors in avoiding gastrointestinal infections. Long-chain omega-3 fats and creatine monohydrate may reduce the severity of traumatic brain injuries, though supportive evidence is largely from animal models or based on head injury biomarkers in humans. Nutritional needs will ultimately vary depending on cycling discipline (road, track, cyclocross, mountain, and BMX), training and competitions loads, lifestyle, and environmental factors. Elite and professional cyclists often deal with injuries and illnesses that can lead to interruptions in training and temporary reductions in performance. This Union Cycliste Internationale-endorsed paper reviews the evidence for nutrition in the prevention and management of four types of injuries and illnesses that are commonly experienced by elite and professional cyclists: skin-related injuries (abrasions, lacerations, etc.), sports-related concussions, upper respiratory tract infections (URTIs), and gastrointestinal (GI) illnesses and disturbances. Skin-related injuries are often reported as the most common type of acute injury experienced by cyclists. Unfortunately, little-to-no cycling-specific research has been conducted to evaluate the role of nutrition in managing skin injuries, meaning that any recommendations must be based on indirect evidence. Provision of dietary energy to facilitate wound healing and repair may need to be individualized, based on the extent/severity of the skin injury, any associated interruptions in training and reductions in exercise energy expenditure, and any measured changes in resting metabolic rate. Adequate provision of protein (≥1.5 g·kg<sup>−1</sup>·day<sup>−1</sup>) and meeting minimal recommended intakes for micronutrients is, in general, important for healing of skin injuries/wounds. Collagen peptides and high-dose vitamin C supplementation enhance connective tissue repair and wound healing in animal models, but evidence in healthy human populations is scarce. To reduce the risk and/or severity of URTIs, a combination of dietary, lifestyle, and supplementation issues should be addressed. Strategies should include consuming carbohydrates and protein to support training demands, regular monitoring and adequate intake of key micronutrients (vitamin D and iron), interventions to minimize or reduce psychological stress, and improving sleep hygiene. In addition, supplementation with probiotics, polyphenols, vitamin C, and zinc lozenges may be of value in some cases, albeit with mixed or inconsistent evidence. If cyclists decide to use high-dose antioxidant supplementation as a strategy to prevent or manage URTIs, we recommend they take a cautious, periodized approach and consider limiting use to periods when they are at higher risk of URTI, and then discontinue supplementation. Due to a variety of ethical and practical issues in carrying out relevant research, only limited and circumstantial evidence is available to support the role of nutrition in preventing and managing sports-related concussions. Animal data and some human data indirectly support possible benefits of long-chain omega-3 fatty acids and creatine supplementation. Supplementing with 2-4 g/day of omega-3 fatty acids is a relatively low-risk strategy that may be warranted in athletes at risk of head trauma, at least among those with poor omega-3 status as confirmed via blood testing. Creatine supplementation also has some limited/circumstantial support for the prevention and mitigation of traumatic brain injury. Given that creatine monohydrate is generally safe in healthy athletes, cyclists at high risk of head trauma may consider a loading regimen (4 × 5 g/day for 5–6 days followed with 2–5 g/day) or, if they are concerned about weight gain, employing a gradual dosing protocol (e.g., 2–3 g/day for 6 weeks). Nutritional strategies for preventing and managing GI problems include short-term diets low in fiber and FODMAPs (fermentable oligosaccharides, disaccharides, monosaccharides, and polyols), regular gut training, and ingesting maltodextrin/glucose–fructose mixtures when consuming carbohydrate at high rates during exercise. Consuming ice slurry or cold beverages has equivocal evidence in preventing GI symptoms but is prudent in hot environments, given potential benefits to other aspects of physiology and performance. Individualizing doses and delivery forms of performance supplements like caffeine and sodium bicarbonate is also recommended. Although probiotics are commonly taken to reduce GI symptoms, evidence to date is mixed. Strategies to prevent infectious GI illnesses, include practicing good personal hygiene, avoiding sharing bottles/cups/utensils, and selecting foods and beverages that are prepackaged if the quality/safety of locally prepared food is questionable. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
