TY  - JOUR
AB  - As a group, cyclists tend to have lower bone mass compared with other athletes, and nonathlete controls which may have negative consequences for fracture risk and longer term bone health. There are several cycling-specific factors that may contribute to this finding. These include, the low-impact, repetitive load nature of cycling which provides limited stimulus for bone metabolism; metabolic perturbations that occur following prolonged cycling that may lead to a transient increase in the rate of bone resorption; and nutritional factors, including low energy availability or nutrient inadequacy. Furthermore, many endurance cyclists are naturally lean and light, which may result from both genetic predisposition and long-term training adaptations. In turn, they may also have lower bone mass. None of these factors stand out as the primary cause of the low bone mass reported in cyclists but, instead, may combine to create a "perfect storm" to challenge cyclist bone health. Given these multiple potential challenges to bone health, a multifaceted approach may be required to combat them. On an individual level, nutritional strategies, including ensuring adequate energy, carbohydrate, protein, calcium, vitamin D, and other important micronutrients may be key to protecting cyclist bone health. Integrating brief bouts of high-impact loading within the training regimen and ensuring adequate sleep and recovery are also recommended. The onus should not remain solely on the individual, however, and cycling teams and organizational bodies have a key role to play in providing screening and educational strategies to support cyclists bone health. Exercise and sports participation are usually considered beneficial for bone health, and it is often assumed that athletes have stronger, more resilient bones than nonathletes. This is not necessarily the case, however, and participation in some sports has been linked to reduced bone density. Cycling is one such example. Research shows that cyclists, particularly high-level road cyclists, tend to have lower bone mass than athletes in other sports, and even compared with nonathletes. This is concerning because less dense bones are more prone to fracture, and young adults with low BMD may also have an increased risk of developing longer term health problems, such as osteoporosis, later in life. In this article, we discuss a number of factors that may contribute to low bone density in cyclists. Bone responds best to high-impact, multidirectional loads. In contrast, cycling, and particularly road cycling, is a low-impact activity involving repetitive movements, meaning it provides limited stimulus to strengthen bone. Long rides can also trigger short-term metabolic changes that increase bone breakdown. Adequate fueling is also critical, not only to support performance but also for bone health. Nutritional factors key to protecting bone include sufficient energy availability, along with adequate intakes of bone-important nutrients including calcium, vitamin D, carbohydrate, and protein. Yet, cyclists are considered a high-risk group for low energy availability and potentially for REDs (Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport). This is partly due to the very high energy demands of cycle training and competition but also from the perception that a lower body mass can improve performance—a belief that can encourage restrictive eating behaviors and potential health problems if taken too far. Many competitive cyclists are also naturally quite lean and light—traits that may be useful when climbing but which are also linked with lower bone mass. None of these factors on their own stand out as the sole, or even primary, reason for low bone mass in cyclists. Instead, we believe they may combine and interact to create a "perfect storm" for cyclist bone health. Tackling these multiple challenges requires a multifaceted approach. At the individual level, cyclists should prioritize adequate energy intake and meet targets for key bone-supporting nutrients, including carbohydrate, protein, calcium, and vitamin D. Adding short bouts of high-impact activity to training and ensuring adequate sleep and recovery may also be important to protecting bone health. The onus should not remain solely on the individual, however, and cycling teams and organizational bodies have important roles to play in providing screening and educational strategies to support cyclist bone health. Fostering healthy cultures around body weight management and discouraging excessively restrictive eating behaviors could also go a long way in supporting rider well-being and performance, both now and in the future. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
