AB  - The article examines the role and reliability of fitness trackers—wearable devices that monitor physical activity, health metrics, and sleep patterns—in cycling and general exercise. While these devices provide accessible data such as heart rate and estimated VO2max that can help shape training and motivate users, their accuracy varies, with some metrics often overestimated compared to laboratory tests. Experts advise using fitness trackers as guides rather than definitive sources, emphasizing the importance of balancing data insights with personal intuition to avoid over-reliance and potential distraction. A case study of a club cyclist highlights the benefits of continuous, passive monitoring for managing training load and recovery. Extracted from the article
