AB  - This study aimed to examine if an association exists between salivary hormones, subjective markers of recovery, training load (TL) and Global Positioning Systems (GPS) metrics in elite male Gaelic football players. Thirteen elite male Gaelic football players provided bi-weekly salivary cortisol and Immunoglobulin A samples, subjective markers of recovery (fatigue levels, stress levels, mood, sleep quality and muscle soreness) and session rating of perceived exertion over 10 weeks covering pre-season (weeks 1 - 4) and in-season competition (weeks 5 - 10). GPS was used to determine total distance (TD) and highspeed running (HSR). A moderate inverse association was identified between TD and cortisol (r = -0.577) and Immunoglobulin A (IgA) (r = -0.580) levels. A moderate inverse association was observed between HSR, cortisol (r = -0.613) and IgA (r = -0.611) levels. An increase in perceived stress showed a moderate correlation with increased cortisol (r = 0.661) and a strong inverse correlation with IgA (r = -0.735). Perceived fatigue significantly correlated with cortisol (r = 0.632). A moderate correlation was observed with perceived sleep, cortisol (r = 0.635), and IgA (r = 0.578). A moderate association was identified between the same week's TL and cortisol (r = 0.579, p = 0.05). A moderate inverse association was found between the same week's TL and IgA (r = -0.584, p = 0.05). This study is the first in Gaelic football to measure salivary cortisol and IgA levels bi-weekly over ten weeks. The results show that subjective monitoring is sensitive to change at a hormonal level and should be used in conjunction with objective data. Furthermore, external metrics should be periodised to avoid excessive TL that could compromise immune function. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
