AB  - Although dietary fiber is widely recognized for its health benefits in the general population, including reduced risk of cardiovascular disease and improved metabolic regulation, its role in athletic performance and recovery remains comparatively underexplored. Current sports nutrition guidelines lack specific recommendations for fiber intake, despite evidence linking adequate consumption to gut microbiome stability, immune modulation, and body composition optimization. Athletes face unique physiological demands that influence gastrointestinal tolerance, nutrient absorption, and energy availability, particularly under high training loads. Although excessive fiber intake may lead to gastrointestinal discomfort or reduced caloric intake, moderate and periodized consumption has been associated with enhanced immune function, improved energy metabolism, and preservation of skeletal muscle mass. Recent findings suggest potential benefits in attenuating exercise-induced inflammation and regulating substrate utilization. Nevertheless, observational data indicate that many athletes fail to meet general population intake targets, often due to precompetition dietary restrictions or concerns about digestive comfort. This review critically synthesizes current evidence on the physiological impacts of dietary fiber in athletic populations, focusing on gastrointestinal health, immune function, body composition, and performance outcomes. It further outlines practical, evidence-based strategies to optimize intake according to individual needs and sport-specific demands, including fiber periodization, source selection, and gradual adaptation. Dietary fiber is widely known for its role in supporting digestive health and reducing the risk of chronic diseases such as heart disease and diabetes. However, its importance for athletes has received much less attention. This paper explores how fiber affects the health, recovery, and performance of athletes and offers practical ways to include it safely and effectively in sports nutrition. Athletes have unique nutritional needs because of their high training volumes and energy demands. Their diet differs from that of the general population, particularly before and during competitions, to minimize gastrointestinal discomfort. As a result, many athletes consume less fiber than recommended. Although temporarily lowering fiber intake before training sessions may help prevent bloating or cramping during exercise, consistently low fiber intake can negatively affect gut health, immune function, and energy metabolism. Dietary fiber refers to the parts of plant foods such as fruits, vegetables, grains, and legumes that the human body cannot digest. These fibers are classified into two main categories. Soluble fibers dissolve in water and can help lower blood cholesterol and stabilize blood sugar, whereas insoluble fibers do not dissolve in water but add bulk to the stool and help prevent constipation. Both types are valuable for athletes, although they act through different biological mechanisms. Dietary fiber supports athletic health by nourishing beneficial bacteria in the gut. These bacteria produce short-chain fatty acids, which help protect the intestinal barrier, reduce inflammation, and provide additional energy for muscle cells. In line with this, adequate fiber intake contributes to both gut and muscle health. A diet rich in fiber may stabilize energy flow, strengthen immune defenses, and boost recovery from intense exercise. Furthermore, foods that are high in fiber cause slower and more stable increases in blood sugar and insulin levels. For athletes, this can promote steady energy release, greater fat oxidation during endurance exercise, and more efficient use of stored glycogen. This metabolic flexibility supports endurance and helps delay fatigue. Despite these benefits, fiber intake must be adjusted carefully. Excessive fiber intake, especially close to training or competition, can lead to stomach discomfort, reduced absorption of nutrients such as protein and fat, and lower total energy intake. These effects can be counterproductive for athletes who need large amounts of energy. Therefore, instead of following a fixed daily target, athletes are encouraged to practice fiber periodization, meaning that fiber intake should vary depending on training phases, goals, and individual tolerance. During competitions, athletes experience increased exposure to crowds and environmental stressors, including temperature, altitude, and humidity, which collectively challenge immune regulation beyond the effects of physical exertion alone. Certain types of fiber, such as beta-glucans found in oats and barley, can strengthen immune responses and reduce postexercise inflammation. They appear to "train" immune cells to react more effectively to physical stress and environmental challenges. Moreover, a healthy and diverse gut microbiota, which depends on adequate fiber intake, contributes to faster recovery and improved resistance to illness. Another benefit of dietary fiber is its potential influence on body composition. Studies show that higher fiber intake is often associated with lower body fat and greater muscle mass. This may be due to better insulin sensitivity, more stable blood glucose levels, and the effects of short-chain fatty acids on muscle metabolism. Fiber-rich foods also promote satiety, which can help athletes manage body weight or reduce fat mass while maintaining muscle tissue. Despite these advantages, research indicates that many athletes still consume less fiber than recommended. Average intakes typically range between 20 and 30 g per day, depending on the type of sport, energy requirements, and dietary patterns. For instance, endurance athletes generally reduce fiber intake because of gastrointestinal sensitivity, whereas strength and team-sport athletes such as rugby players tend to consume more, partly due to their higher total food intake. To optimize health and performance, several practical strategies can be implemented. Fiber intake should be increased gradually over several weeks to allow the digestive system to adapt and minimize gastrointestinal discomfort. A combination of soluble and insoluble fibers from fruits, vegetables, whole grains, oats, legumes, and seeds is recommended to ensure diverse nutritional and metabolic benefits. Adequate hydration is also essential as dehydration can exacerbate gastrointestinal symptoms and diminish the positive effects of fiber on digestion. In the 24–48 hr before high-intensity training or competition, athletes may benefit from reducing insoluble and gas-forming fibers such as bran and beans to prevent bloating and discomfort. By contrast, during recovery periods, focusing on soluble fiber sources can further support immune function and antioxidant defenses and promoting faster recovery. In conclusion, fiber intake deserves more attention in sports nutrition. When consumed in adequate amounts and at appropriate times, dietary fiber can promote a healthy gut environment, support metabolic efficiency, strengthen immunity, and help maintain a favorable body composition. The ideal approach is to consider 25–35 g of fiber per day as a reasonable range rather than a strict target, within a flexible and individualized plan that accounts for training cycles, energy needs, and digestive tolerance. As scientific research continues, dietary fiber may prove to be an essential yet often underestimated factor in achieving both optimal athletic performance and long-term health. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
