AB  - The article focuses on the concept of allostatic load, defined as the cumulative physiological strain resulting from the body's ongoing adaptation to various stressors, including training, work pressure, poor sleep, and emotional strain. It explains that while allostasis—the process of adapting to challenges—is essential for fitness improvements, excessive or prolonged stress without adequate recovery can impair mood, immunity, energy, and performance, particularly in cyclists. Signs of elevated allostatic load include muted heart rate response, increased perceived effort, disrupted sleep, and frequent illness. Experts recommend monitoring internal markers such as heart rate variability (HRV), prioritizing recovery, and ensuring proper nutrition to manage allostatic load and maintain healthy adaptation. Extracted from the article
