TY  - JOUR
AB  - Rest redistribution (RR) is an innovative training method that restructures rest periods into shorter, more frequent intervals while maintaining the overall rest duration. For example, a traditional set with 3 sets of 6 repetitions and 180 s of rest between sets can be reconfigured in RR to 9 sets of 2 repetitions with 45 s rest, both totalling 360 s of rest. The use of RR has shown potential to improve training effectiveness by reducing fatigue and perceived exertion, enabling athletes to sustain higher intensities, velocities, and power outputs throughout a session. This literature review examines aerobic endurance training methods, with an emphasis on improvements in VO<sub>2max,</sub> and explores how set structure in both aerobic and resistance training impacts physiological and perceptual responses. The review searched PubMed, Google Scholar, and ProQuest for studies from 1991 to 2023 using keywords like "aerobic capacity," "interval training," and "rest redistribution," resulting in 35 relevant, peer-reviewed papers. It highlights the effectiveness of continuous and interval training methods, particularly high-intensity interval training and maximal aerobic speed (MAS) protocols, in improving VO<sub>2max </sub>and endurance performance. Although RR has proven effective in resistance training, its application to aerobic conditioning requires further investigation. Potential benefits of RR include reduced fatigue and perceptual effort while maintaining or enhancing performance and training adaptations. For team sport athletes, protocols such as 4 x (6 min at 90% MAS, with 20s RR every 90s) and long-distance runners, 3 x (10 min at 85% MAS, with 30s RR every 2 min) are recommended. Incorporating RR into aerobic conditioning represents a promising approach for optimising training adaptations, improving performance and reducing fatigue during sessions. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
