TY  - JOUR
AB  - Breath control training is crucial in swimming, especially in shortdistance races where swimmers need to optimize their breathing to enhance performance. This study investigated the impact of breathing frequency control training on physical abilities and performance levels among sprint swimmers. The study aimed to examine the effect of breathing frequency control training on maximum speed, explosive power, speed endurance, and performance levels (50m freestyle, 100m freestyle, 50m butterfly) among young sprint swimmers. The researcher used the experimental approach with a single-group design, employing pre- and post-measurements. The sample consisted of 30 male swimmers from the 2009 team at the Shooting Club, 6th of October branch, aged 15 years. The sample was divided into a main sample (20 swimmers) and an exploratory sample (10 swimmers). The results showed statistically significant differences between the preand post-measurements in all physical and performance variables in favor of the post-measurement. The improvement rates ranged from 3.58% to 11.29%. The researcher recommended incorporating breathing frequency control exercises into weekly swimming programs to develop physical abilities related to achievement in sprint swimmers. Additionally, anaerobic training loads should be standardized while considering individual differences. This training method can help swimmers optimize their performance and achieve better results in short-distance races. By adopting breathing frequency control training, swimmers can improve their overall performance and gain a competitive edge in sprint events. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
