AB  - Plyometric jump training (PJT) is a resistance training method which utilizes the stretch-shortening cycle (SSC). Jumps, hops and bounds are examples of PJT movements. Endurance running is a broad term for distances employing submaximal speeds, with the aerobic energy system in predominance. The purpose of this review of the literature was to investigate how PJT impacts endurance running performance. Two electronic databases; SportDiscus and PubMed, were used to find relevant literature, with 12 studies satisfying all inclusion criteria; (1) publication date from 2015-2024, (2) participants defined as "endurance runners" or similar, or participated in a minimum distance of 1500m, (3) articles were available online for electronic download, and (4) studies utilised a training intervention incorporating PJT. When performed in isolation, PJT does not definitively enhance running economy (RE) nor endurance running time trial (TT) performance. When performed concurrently with strength training, however, RE and TT performance are enhanced. For elite endurance runners 2-3 resistance training sessions per week is advised, with 2-4 heavy strength (>80% 1 repetition maximum [1RM]) and 2-4 PJT movements in each session. PJT load can be measured using the number of foot contacts, with 30-120 per session and 100-300 per week recommended. Exercise complexity and running training load are factors to consider for strength and conditioning coaches when programming. To ensure both heavy strength and PJT movements are included, contrast sets are a recommended method. Performing a heavy strength movement such as 5 repetitions at 90% 1RM barbell back squat, preceding a PJT movement such as 5 explosive bodyweight depth jumps, may induce post-activation potentiation (PAP). Power may therefore be maximised, and PJT ground contact time (GCT) minimized. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
