AB  - The application of effective physical conditioning strategies in team sports training are essential factors that assist the development and optimisation of athletic performance. Rugby union is a high-impact team sport involving periods of high-intensity intermittent activities, followed by periods of lower-intensity efforts and rest periods, the demands of which vary considerably between positional groups. The purpose of this investigation was to ascertain the positional physical demands of professional rugby union players, comparing the game demands and training loads. Fifteen male professional rugby union players (seven forwards and eight backs) provided GPS data from 47 games and 96 trainings to ascertain and compare the game demands and training loads. Total distance (5790 m vs 6207 m; p=0.045), distance rate (68 m.min-1 vs 73 m.min-1; p=0.014), and very high-speed running distance (VHSR) (19 m vs 82 m; p<0.001) during games were significantly less for forwards compared with backs. In training, forwards covered significantly less VHSR than backs (17 m vs 27 m; p=0.048). All variables, except VHSR for forwards, were significantly lower in training compared to games for both forwards and backs (p>0.05, with small to extremely large effect sizes). Overall findings of this investigation indicate that the demands of training do not replicate the demands of games, suggesting that training may need to be modified to ensure players are exposed to the game-level demands to enhance physical preparation and potential performance outcomes, either through skill-based drills or conditioning programmes designed to imitate or exceed the running demands of a game. The findings of this investigation may be of value to coaches and practitioners to assist in the development, conditioning, and preparation of professional rugby union players. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
