AB  - Aim: This study aims to examine physiological responses during a gradually increasing running test by simultaneously evaluating heart rate (HR), heart rate variability (HRV), and muscle oxygen saturation (SmO<sub>2</sub>) parameters. Materials and Methods: Sixteen male athletes aged between 18 and 27 years, with at least 3 years of sports experience, participated in the study. The average age was 19.25 ± 2.21 years, average height was 180±7.87 cm, average weight was 78.3±14.6 kg, and average body fat percentage was 11.8±5.85. HR, HRV, and SmO<sub>2</sub> values were recorded during the resting, running, and recovery phases throughout the test. The running protocol started at a speed of 10 km/h and increased by 1 km/h every minute until exhaustion. Recording continued for 5 minutes post-run. Results: During the gradually increasing running test, it was observed that heart rate (HR) increased while SmO<sub>2</sub> levels decreased. Throughout the recovery period after running, both measurement parameters tended to approach resting values. Conclusion: These findings indicate that the joint assessment of HR, HRV, and SmO<sub>2</sub> is significant for a holistic analysis of the physiological responses to exercise. In performance evaluation, measurements such as heart rate (HR), heart rate variability (HRV), and intramuscular oxygen saturation (SmO<sub>2</sub>) hold an important place alongside running tests. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
