AB  - The article investigates the phase angle (PhA), a non-invasive parameter related to cellular functional status and muscle quality, as a biomarker to predict performance in youth swimmers over a 15-week macrocycle, focusing on the taper phase. Twenty young swimmers were evaluated at five different time points, measuring PhA, body composition, muscle strength, stroke rate, and speed in 25-meter events, as well as training load. The results indicate that PhA increased during the taper, correlating significantly with performance, regardless of training load. It is concluded that the phase angle can be a useful tool to monitor and personalize training, assessing competition readiness in youth swimmers. Extracted from the article
