AB  - This article examines the anaerobic speed reserve (ASR)--the difference between maximal sprint speed (MSS) and maximal aerobic speed (MAS)--as a framework for individualizing high-intensity interval training (HIIT), addressing shortcomings of MAS-only approaches. By utilizing the MSS:MAS ratio, ASR enables classification of athletes into speed-dominant, hybrid, or endurance-dominant profiles, facilitating tailored HIIT prescriptions that optimize performance across diverse physiological capacities (3, 6). The study synthesizes ASR's theoretical basis, profiling utility, and practical implementation, including intensity calibration and work-to-rest ratios, demonstrating its capacity to standardize physiological demands and enhance training efficacy (7, 8). However, limitations such as the reliance on precise MAS and MSS measurements and ASR's incomplete capture of team sport demands (e.g., soccer's directional changes) necessitate rigorous testing protocols and supplementary monitoring (3, 13). Integration of heart rate variability (HRV), rating of perceived exertion (RPE), and countermovement jump (CMJ) metrics mitigates these constraints, addressing individual response variability influenced by factors like training age and genetics (10, 12, 17). ASR proves particularly effective for supramaximal intensities, yet its full potential requires a holistic approach, balancing training specificity with athlete wellbeing (7). ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
