AB  - Aim: The aim of this study is to determine the effects of different intervention methods in small-sided games (SSGs) on locomotor and mechanical outputs and to comparatively evaluate these methods. Method: The study included 10 licensed players from the Beşiktaş U-17 team. The players participated in four different SSG formats over a twoweek period. These game formats were: zone transition with external joker players (BGSHDJ), only external joker players (SHDJ), zone transition with goal-oriented play (BGKAO), and only goal-oriented play (SKAO). The locomotor variables analyzed included total distance covered, walking, low-speed running, moderate-speed running, high-speed running, very high-speed running, and maximum speed. The mechanical variables analyzed were the number of accelerations and decelerations. Data were analyzed using ANOVA. Results: SSGs conducted with the BGSHDJ intervention resulted in significantly higher values for total distance covered (1664±132 m), moderate-speed running (467.4±96 m), high-speed running (132±63), accelerations (42.0±8.1), and decelerations (35.9±9.4) compared to the other methods. In contrast, the distance covered by walking (412±35 m) was significantly lower (p<0.05). Conclusion:The BGSHDJ intervention had a significant impact on locomotor and mechanical outputs in SSGs. These findings suggest that different SSG formats can be effectively utilized to optimize the physical performance of football players. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
