AB  - We compared the front-crawl kinematics, arm-stroke sufficiency, and internal load of a 2 km test (time-trial) between pool and open water swimming. Fifteen trained male Master swimmers (age = 39.7 ± 8.3 years), who competed predominantly in open water swimming events and engaged in regular swimming pool training, were assessed at specific range distances (SPLIT 1:35-45 m; SPLIT 2:685-695 m; SPLIT 3:1335-1345 m; and SPLIT 4:1985-1995 m) during both conditions. The rate of perceived exertion was registered immediately after each 2 km trial. The internal load was calculated for further analysis. Results revealed that swimming speed, stroke length, and arm stroke efficiency were higher at the swimming pool (p < .05; np² = .49 to .81). However, the stroke rate was notably higher in open water (p < .001; np² = .76). The rate of perceived exertion was similar between both conditions. Nevertheless, the internal load was higher in open water conditions (p< .01; d = 1.40). These findings suggest that the 2 km test elicits different responses (kinematics, arm-stroke efficiency, and internal load) due to environmental conditions (swimming pool or open water). Coaches should modify training programs for open water swimming by adding more sessions in open water environments, focusing on series to improve swimming technique. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
