AB  - This article focuses on the influence of menstrual cycle (MC) phases on physiological responses in trained ultra-endurance female athletes. In a study involving seven ultramarathon runners, no significant differences were found across early follicular, late follicular, and mid-luteal MC phases in key physiological measures such as resting metabolic rate, heart rate variability, VO2peak, and running performance, despite over half reporting perceived performance declines related to menstruation. The findings suggest physiological performance remains stable across MC phases, highlighting the importance of combining objective monitoring with symptom tracking in female athletes. The authors recommend larger studies with hormonal verification to better understand MC effects on ultra-endurance performance. Extracted from the article
