AB  - Aerobic capacity plays a key role in preparing athletes for the physical demands of team-based field sports (TBFS). However, there is limited evidence on the effect that aerobic capacity has on the risk of injury. Considering the prevalence of lower body injuries in TBFS, all potential means of reducing injury risk should be explored. Therefore, it was the aim of this review to identify whether aerobic capacity influences injury risk in male TBFS athletes. A literature search was conducted using the SPORTDiscus, Pubmed, and Medline databases. six peer reviewed articles relevant to the topic of interest were identified and four more were included from external sources for a total of ten articles. Research exploring the relationship between aerobic capacity and injury risk in rugby found that there was limited association. Conversely, greater aerobic capacity resulted in a decreased risk of injury in elite soccer, Gaelic football, and Australian rules football (ARF) players compared to those with poorer aerobic capacity. Greater aerobic capacity also allows soccer and Gaelic football players to tolerate greater training and high-speed running loads without injury compared to those with poorer aerobic capacity. In conclusion, greater levels of aerobic capacity result in a decreased risk of injury in ARF, soccer, and Gaelic football players, but research relating to rugby athletes is less compelling. It is recommended that practitioners prioritise the development of aerobic capacity in the pre-season and monitor athletes' aerobic fitness when returning from injury to reduce the risk of injury. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
