AB  - The article focuses on the impact of dehydration on endurance exercise performance, highlighting the need for further research on sex differences and the potential for athletes to adapt to repeated dehydration. It notes that while dehydration is known to impair performance, most studies have primarily involved male participants, leaving gaps in understanding how females, who have different physiological responses, are affected. Additionally, the article discusses real-world observations suggesting that some athletes can perform well despite significant dehydration, raising questions about physiological adaptations to repeated exposure. Future research should prioritize well-designed studies that consider sex, training status, and exercise modalities to better inform hydration guidelines for diverse athletic populations. Extracted from the article
