AB  - The article discusses a preliminary analysis of the relationship between training load and free triiodothyronine (FT3) levels in female ultramarathon trail runners preparing for a 100-mile endurance race. FT3, a thyroid hormone involved in metabolic regulation, is considered a primary indicator of Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (REDs). The study examined 14 female participants, aged 40.8±5.5 years, with 15.1±5.7 years of racing experience, at the 2024 Western States Endurance Race. FT3 was measured at three time points (16 weeks, 8 weeks, and 0 weeks prior to the race), while training load was quantified as weekly distance, time, and internal load. Results showed no significant changes in FT3 over time, despite a 14.3 km increase in weekly distance, 2.2 hours in weekly time, and a 25.8% rise in internal load between the 8-week and 0-week intervals. The findings suggest that short-term increases in training load alone may not suppress thyroid function in this population. Extracted from the article
