AB  - Training control allows coaches to have accurate information about the load on planned training tasks. The present study aims to analyse the external load (distance, high speed running - >21km/h, Sprint - >24km/h, maximal speed, accelerations and decelerations) in 3x3 small-sided games (SSG) with and without additional players (1 joker and 2 pivots). Eighteen Under-19 players (16.92 ± 0.65 years; 67.61 ± 5.45 kg, 178 ± 5.12 cm), who compete in the 1st District Division of Setúbal, participated in the study. Data collection was carried out using WIMU GPS devices. The playing space is 20 m x 15 m (300 m²). Each team attacks and defends 2 mini-goals. The SSG 3x3 and 3x3+3 lasted 4 min with 4 min of recovery in between. The data were analysed through descriptive statistics, t-test methodology (p<0.05) and the d-Cohen effect size. The results demonstrate that there are no statistically significant differences between the two SSGs in the variables distance, speed zones, maximum speed, accelerations (<3 and >3 m/s²), and decelerations (<3 and >3 m/s²). In all these variables, the size of the effect of using additional players on the external load is trivial (0-0.2) and small (0.2-0.6). Statistically significant differences (p<0.01; d=-1.18) were found between SSG 3x3 (140.08 ± 18.64) and 3x3+3 (160.36 ± 14.47) in the variable speed 0-6 km/h. We observed in all variables that pivot and joker players have lower external load values compared to the other players. In conclusion, the 3x3 SSG with and without additional players doesn’t present differences in the external load variables studied, except for the speed of 0-6 km/h. The two SSGs do not allow players to reach values of high-speed running (>21km/h) and sprint (>24km/h), and do not promote high-intensity accelerations and decelerations. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
