TY  - JOUR
AB  - This study aimed to enhance the lower extremity strength of an elite short-distance track cyclist with chronic injuries. Functional training were conducted to identify the athlete's physical dysfunctions that constrain improvements in lower-limb strength, and a corresponding training-cycle plan for the Asian Games preparation period was subsequently designed. Guided by the "Rehabilitation-Performance Training Integration (RPTI)" training philosophy, the initial phase focused on functional training to alleviate chronic lumbar, back, and knee pain. progressively improving physical function and laying the foundation for high-load strength training. During the sub-sequent 12-week strength training phase, velocity-based training (VBT) was employed to precisely modulate training load and optimize training efficiency. As a result, the athlete's lower extremity strength and explosive power improved substantially, with a 22.81% increase in 1-repetition maximum (1RM) squat strength, a 38. 58% rise in maximal power output, and a 30. 66% enhancement in maximal power in 80 kg barbell power clean. Ultimately, the athlete achieved breakthrough performance in the team sprint event at the Asian Games. This case study provides a practical reference for the scientific training of elite athletes with chronic pain and functional limitations. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
