AB  - The article reports on a prospective observational analysis examining the influence of the menstrual cycle (MC) phase on mood states, training load, and sleep parameters in collegiate female rowers. The study involved fifteen eumenorrheic female rowers, who completed the Profile of Mood States (POMS) twice weekly and reported their menstrual phase, sleep duration and quality, self-motivation, training distance, and session rating of perceived exertion (RPE). Results indicated that depression scores were significantly greater during the luteal phase compared to the follicular phase, while other POMS subscales and total mood disturbance (TMD) did not differ by phase. Additionally, RPE positively correlated with tension, anger, and confusion, and sleep quantity was inversely correlated with these negative mood states. Across the season, fatigue, confusion, and TMD decreased, suggesting improved psychological adaptation to training demands. The findings highlight the importance of individualized monitoring of mood and menstrual health to support psychological resilience and performance in female athletes. Extracted from the article
