AB  - The article discusses a study comparing physiological thresholds in elite female rowers using near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS), heart rate variability (HRV), blood lactate (BLa), and ventilatory measures. Seven elite female rowers completed a graded exercise test on a rowing ergometer, and thresholds were determined through various methods, including piecewise breakpoint analysis and visual inspection. The results showed that lactate threshold 1 (LT1) occurred at lower wattage than ventilatory threshold 1 (VT1) and lactate breakpoint 1 (LTBP1), while HRV-based threshold 1 (HRV-BP1) occurred at lower wattage than LT1. The study concluded that NIRS and HRV provide practical, noninvasive estimates of physiological thresholds that complement traditional testing for training monitoring. Extracted from the article
