AB  - The aim of the study was to stablish relationships between post-exercise heart rate variability (HRV) values and internal training load (TL) components measured by three Training Impulse (TRIMP) methods in youth triathletes. TRIMP methods were related with each other (r = 0,970 to 0,983, R2 = 0,940 to 0,967) but showed significant differences in mean values (p < 0,01). Training significantly reduced HRV (p < 0,01) with a significant time effect on HRV recovery (p < 0,01). Post5 HRV reduction has a negative relationship to exercise intensity, volume at >90% HRres, and TRIMP values (r = -0,302 to -0,495, p < 0,01) and a positive relationship with volume at <50% HRres (r = 0.488, p < 0,01). Immediate postexercise HRV reduction can provide valuable information for evaluation of physiological effects at intervallic or continuous training in youth triathletes. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
