AB  - The article reports on a case study analyzing wearable data to track weight loss and cardiometabolic health improvements in an adult male over three years. The individual, aged 27-30, lost 30.6% of his body weight (from 124.7 to 86.5 kg) and completed an Ironman 70.3 triathlon, with daily metrics like resting heart rate, heart rate variability, sleep, and physical activity showing significant improvements. Clinical biomarkers, including fasting glucose, triglycerides, LDL, and HDL, also improved, and post-weight-loss assessments indicated above-average fitness levels. The study highlights the potential of wearable technology in monitoring long-term lifestyle changes and their health benefits. Extracted from the article
