AB  - The article discusses the various metrics that cyclists can measure while riding, including time, distance, average speed, elevation, heart rate, cadence, and power, as well as additional biological metrics like heart rate variability (HRV) and blood sugar levels. It highlights the growing capabilities of wearable technology in tracking these metrics and its potential to enhance understanding of human performance. However, the author emphasizes the importance of enjoying the ride itself, suggesting that not all cyclists need to focus on extensive data collection, as personal enjoyment and practical considerations, like returning in time for meals, remain paramount. Extracted from the article
