AB  - The Leadership Development Program (LDP) is designed to prepare Cadets for future Military Commissions. A large portion of the LDP is physical training, preparing the Cadets for the vigorous Cadet Summer Program (CSP). Ruck March (RM) training is an important aspect of the conditioning program. Previous research has shed light on poor sleep habits in Corp of Cadets that compelled us to further investigate this topic. There is limited research in the literature on sleep habits in Military personnel, especially in college aged Cadets. The purpose of this study was to examine the sleep habits (amount of sleep and stress score (HRV) 7-days leading up to a 6-mile and 8-mile loaded Ruck March. Fourteen cadets (10 males and 4 females) from the Leadership Development Program participated in this study. A timed and required 6-mile and 8-mile RM were analyzed on the 8th day preceded by 7 days of data collection on sleep habits and stress scores. Cadets wore a Garmin Forerunner watch 24-hours per day, collecting sleep time and stress scores. The wearable device was also used during the 6-mile and 8-mile RM with the Running Dynamics Pod. Findings conclude that partial sleep deprivation in cadets did not have an influence on the 6-mile and the 8-mile loaded ruck march finish time. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
