AB  - This systematic scoping review aimed to synthesise the physiological and kinematic determinants of butterfly swimming performance. Searches were conducted in PubMed, Scopus, Web of Science and EBSCO, as well as in the proceedings of the Biomechanics and Medicine in Swimming Symposia and of the International Society of Biomechanics in Sports. Database searches identified 645 articles, which were supplemented by manual searches, snowballing citation tracking and consultation with external experts. After screening, 138 articles met the eligibility criteria, of which 70 were included in the synthesis. Among them, 50 focused on kinematic variables, 12 on physiological variables, and eight adopted a biophysical perspective. It was observed that butterfly kinematic variables, such as stroke rate, stroke length, and intracyclic velocity variation, influence energy cost and speed, as well as inter-limbs coordination, body undulation, and breathing strategy. The combination of these variables directly influences swimming propulsion and active drag, resulting in high energy expenditure and propelling efficiency in butterfly. Therefore, the analysis of butterfly swimming should use integrated approaches that combine kinematic and physiological assessments. In addition, it was identified that long-term training interventions are limited, standardised training load protocols that reflect actual competition demands have not been systematically evaluated, and biophysical assessments are less frequently applied in butterfly swimming research. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
