AB  - The purpose of this study was to analyze the use of heart rate variability (HRV) as a feedback mechanism for performance through load training control. Seven male amateur elite triathlon athletes of national and international level (age, 42.0 ± 4.0 yrs; weight, 79.5 ± 11.6 kg; height, 177.0 ± 8.0 cm) had their time domain HRV readings taken immediately prior and after an indoor cycling training session as part of their normal training routine. The descriptive data are shown as mean ± standard deviation while inferential analysis was made by t test (P<0.05). Mean values before (PRE) and after (POST) the cycling session were, respectively: MEANRR 1018.55 ms (± 135.80 ms) and 822.52 ms (± 93.12 ms; Δ8.0%), SDNN 60.22 ms (± 21.75 ms) and 50.57 ms (± 15.80 ms; Δ8.3%), MEANHR 60.06 ms (± 8.05 ms) and 74.0 ms (± 8.09 ms; Δ12.3%), RMSSD 54.07 ms (± 33.39 ms) and 45.84 ms (± 14.66 ms; Δ8.4%), and pNN50 27.44 ms (± 21.78 ms) and 13.14 ms (± 12.31 ms; Δ4.7%). SDNN and RMSSD parameters changed significantly, thus indicating that an indoor cycling session triggered sufficient stress to alter HRV in its time domain. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
