AB  - The article discusses a study examining whether a multi-parameter profile could be identified in elite football players prior to non-contact injuries, using GPS, creatine kinase (CPK), and wellness questionnaire measures. The research involved monitoring 39 injuries in an elite men's football squad over a competitive season, analyzing total distance, high-metabolic-load (HML) distance, high-speed running (HSR) distance, CPK levels, and wellness indicators like fatigue, sleep quality, and muscle soreness. A composite Injury Risk Index (IRI) was developed, incorporating total distance, CPK, and fatigue score, with higher IRI values correlating to a greater likelihood of injury. The findings suggest that injuries are often preceded by reduced training load and rising CPK, fatigue, and soreness, alongside slight declines in sleep quality. Extracted from the article
