AB  - The purpose of this study was to assess the relationship between the Acute:Chronic Workload Ratio (ACWR) and injury risk in sub-elite Australian Rules (AR) football using measures of physical load derived from GPS. Across three seasons, 3941 data files were collected from 111 players with these data used to calculate the ACWR for total distance (TD), high-speed running (HSR) distance (13.0km/h - 25.0km/h) and sprint distance (SD) (>25.0km/h). The relationship between ACWR and the occurrence of injury in both current and subsequent weeks were examined using a Binominal Logistic Regression. Additionally, both Odds Ratios (OR) and the Relative Risk (RR) were calculated to quantify the association between ACWR and injury. No significant relationships were found between the ACWR and the occurrence of an injury. The likelihood of an injury occurring in the current (OR=1.27, 95%CI 0.54-3.04, p=0.58) or preceding week (OR=1.47, 95%CI 0.62-3.53, p=0.38) was not affected by the TD ACWR. The likelihood of an injury occurring in the current (OR=1.11, 95%CI 0.44-2.77, p=0.81) or preceding week (OR=1.62, 95%CI 0.65-4.04, p=0.30) was not affected by the HSR ACWR. The likelihood of an injury occurring in the current (OR=0.89, 95%CI 0.41-1.92, p=0.77) or preceding week (OR=0.92, 95%CI 0.42-2.02, p=0.85) was not affected by the SD ACWR. These findings demonstrate that the ACWR does not appear to have the ability to identify injury risk in either the current or subsequent week in a sub-elite setting. As such, caution should be taken by practitioners when using the ACWR as a monitoring tool to identify risk of injury to players in this environment. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
