AB  - The article reports on a study examining heart rate variability (HRV) differences among Division II track and field athletes, including distance runners, sprinters, jumpers, and throwers. HRV, which reflects autonomic nervous system function, was measured over three days using a monitor, with no significant statistical differences found across groups in key parameters like R-R interval, heart rate, or LF/HF ratio. However, large effect sizes were observed, indicating high variability due to athletic specialization. The authors suggest that sport-specific training may not strongly influence HRV in this population, but further research with larger samples is warranted. Extracted from the article
