AB  - A variety of approaches exist attempting to quantify the physical demands athletes are subjected to during intermittent team sports. These approaches range from video-based time-motion analysis, to more contemporary methods through use of global navigation satellite systems (GNSS) technology. The aim of this review was to evaluate the different approaches practitioners could take in order to quantify the physical demands placed upon athletes within intermittent team sports. Electronic databases PUBMED and SPORTDiscus were searched for all time periods up until March 2021. 77 articles met the criteria and were included in this review. This review demonstrated, due to the inclusion of athlete low speed activity during stoppage time when the ball is not in play, match activity profiles expressing average match demands underestimate match play intensities. Advances in GNSS microtechnology now enable practitioners to extract athlete movement activity that relates exclusively to 'active' time when the ball is in play. The use of ball in play data is also useful when attempting to analyse longest continuous playing periods. Research adopting a ball in play approach has been utilised across many intermittent team sports, including rugby league, and has demonstrated significant increases in match intensities (mean speed, low speed activity, high-speed running) when compared to average match demands. In order to report match activity profiles with greater precision, practitioners are advised to establish a ball in play approach to ensure athletes are adequately prepared to perform extended, repeated bouts of high-intensity exercise. Given the unfamiliar nature of gameplay in the current National Rugby League, as a result of the introduction of the '6-again' rule, a ball in play approach may prove valuable toward modernising training prescriptions. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
