AB  - The article focuses on the study by Cassirame et al. regarding Detrended Fluctuation Analysis (DFA) as a method for determining physiological thresholds in exercise intensity through heart rate variability (HRV). It discusses the methodological challenges and influential factors affecting the use of the short-term scaling exponent alpha1 of DFA (DFAa1) in exercise settings, highlighting issues such as signal-to-noise ratio and individual variability. The authors argue for a careful and rigorous methodological approach to DFAa1, emphasizing its theoretical foundations in systems biology and Network Physiology of Exercise (NPE), and suggest that, when applied correctly, DFAa1 could provide valuable insights into endurance exercise monitoring alongside traditional metabolic methods. Extracted from the article
