AB  - Maintaining balance between training stress and recovery is essential in the pursuit of athletic performance; however, expertise-related differences in characteristics of recovery are understudied. This study compared sleep – biologically fundamental recovery with strong effects on sport performance – among athletes of different skill levels. Forty-four competitive, endurance sport athletes (M<sub>age</sub> = 22.0 ± 4.7) recorded the timing, quantity, and quality of their sleep, and training load, over 14 days. Athletes were categorised as elite (i.e., international), pre-elite (i.e., national), or non-elite level (i.e., provincial). In comparison to non-elite athletes, both elite and pre-elite athletes reported falling asleep significantly earlier (Elite: p =.037; Pre-elite: p =.030) spending more time in bed at night (Elite: p =.040; Pre-elite: p =.028), and napping longer during the day (Elite: p =.002; Pre-elite: p =.002). However, elite athletes did not sleep significantly longer than non-elite athletes, and elite athletes reported significantly poorer measures of sleep quality (e.g., sleep efficiency, subjective sleep quality ratings). The pattern of significant findings suggests the behaviours of the elite/pre-elite provided the opportunity for greater sleep, but that elite athletes nonetheless reported sub-optimal to poor sleep quantity and quality. Further, statistically significant group differences were limited in magnitude, potentially limiting practical significance. Despite its potential impact on performance, elite athletes in this sample did not appear to leverage sleep for an expert advantage. We discuss several areas for further work related to recovery and sport expertise. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
