AB  - The article discusses a study examining the relationship between age, competitive experience, and cardiac risk indicators among Ironman triathletes. The research, conducted on 32 participants aged 23–69, analyzed heart rate variability (HRV), heart strain (HS), and time spent in heart rate zones using portable ECG devices. Results showed that age significantly influenced average HRV, with middle-aged to older triathletes (ages 40–44 and >60) exhibiting lower HRV. Limited competitive experience (1–4 races) was associated with lower HRV and higher time spent in heart rate zones 4 and 5. No significant effects were found for age or experience on HS. The study concludes that older age may contribute to increased cardiac risk, while amateur triathletes may face greater cardiac workload compared to more experienced participants. Extracted from the article
