AB  - The article reports on a study comparing wellness and performance metrics of collegiate lacrosse athletes who were naturally cycling (NC) to hormonal contraceptive (HC) users across menstrual cycle phases and varying training loads. The research measured daily wellness scores and external performance metrics, such as total distance, maximum speed, and high-intensity distance, using wearable technology. No significant differences were found between NC and HC users, though seasonal variations and training load influenced stress, mood, and performance. Spring 20-hour training weeks improved performance metrics but reduced wellness indicators, suggesting the need for individualized periodized training. The study emphasizes the importance of tailoring training approaches to optimize both performance and athlete well-being. Extracted from the article
