AB  - The article discusses the influence of locomotor profiles on training adaptations in youth cross-country skiers. Nine regional-level female skiers (13-17 years old) underwent assessments before and after seven weeks of coach-supervised training, including a 6-minute half-Cooper test and a 40-meter sprint, to evaluate changes in maximal aerobic speed (MAS), maximal sprint speed (MSS), and anaerobic speed reserve (ASR). The study found that while maximal aerobic capacity remained stable, sprint performance declined significantly. The locomotor profiles shifted, with more athletes classified as endurance-type, but no clear relationship was observed between profile clustering and changes in MAS or MSS. The results underscore the dynamic nature of locomotor profiles in young athletes and the need for repeated monitoring to tailor training strategies. Extracted from the article
