AB  - This article explains the multiple factors influencing heart rate (HR) variability during sports training, particularly in running. It details how effort intensity, temperature, fatigue, stress, sleep, hydration, altitude, training level, caffeine consumption, as well as illness can alter HR, making its interpretation complex but essential for optimizing progress and avoiding overtraining. Heart rate variability (HRV), measuring the micro-variations between beats, is presented as a key indicator of recovery and adaptation. Finally, the article highlights the limitations of wrist-based measurements compared to chest straps, especially in terms of accuracy during intense or variable efforts. Extracted from the article
