AB  - Introduction: Looking at the literature, the results of studies investigating the effect of different exercise types on heart rate variability (HRV) seem to be contradictory. However, existing studies suggest that HRV may differ in structurally different sports and that different exercise models may have different power outputs even when performed at the same pulse rate. The aim of this study was to investigate the HRV response and muscle oxygen saturation in rowing and track and field athletes after different body region-dominant exercises at similar pulse intervals. Method: Eighteen volunteer male athletes, 10 in rowing and 8 in middle-long distance running, participated in the study. The athletes performed a gradually increasing exercise test on the first day and a 6-minute exercise test at an individually calculated target heart rate on the second day. Prior to the protocols, athletes were fitted with a chest strap with a heart rate monitor and near infrared spectroscopy (NIRS). Heart rate (HR), HRV, and muscle oxygen saturation levels were monitored at rest before, during, and 15 minutes after the test. Results: The target heart rates calculated at the end of the graded exercise test were 164 (± 3.92) bpm in rowers and 161 (± 7.41) bpm in runners. In the 6-minute test, the muscle oxygen saturation level for the biceps brachii muscle decreased from 61.5% (± 3.98) to 46.4% (± 4.17) in rowers and from 61.9% (± 5.39) to 50.7% (± 6.25) in runners. 7% (± 6.25), for the vastus lateralis muscle it decreased from 65.2% (± 4.39) to 54.4% (± 5.84) in rowers and from 63.8% (± 2.87) to 53.1% (± 5.18) in runners. Conclusion: Post-test 15-minute recovery at the applied target heart rate was analyzed with consecutive 30-second periods. A significant difference was found between the mean RR values of rowing and running athletes during recovery. At the end of the test, the duration of reactive hyperemia for the biceps brachii muscle was 222 s (± 101) in runners and 274 s (± 325) in rowers, while for the vastus lateralis muscle it was 305 s (± 174) in runners and 155 s (± 105) in rowers. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
