AB  - The article focuses on the importance of aligning training loads with game demands in professional rugby union to enhance player performance and reduce injury risk. A study conducted by researchers from the University of Tasmania analyzed GPS data from professional players, concluding that training should better replicate game conditions, with specific recommendations for high-speed running distances. Additionally, a literature review by a researcher from Edith Cowan University highlighted that increased preseason training loads correlate with lower injury rates during the competitive season, emphasizing the need for adequate preparation time, especially in light of disruptions like the COVID-19 pandemic. The findings provide practical insights for strength and conditioning coaches to optimize athlete training strategies. Extracted from the article
