AB  - Peaking in cycling refers to aligning an athlete's physical and mental state to achieve optimal performance at a specific event. The process involves a structured training regimen leading up to a tapering period, where training load is reduced to allow for recovery. Research suggests that the hardest training should occur three to five weeks before the target event, with the tapering phase starting one to three weeks prior. The best peaking strategy depends on individual recovery and adaptation profiles, but a general rule is to start tapering two weeks before the event, maintain training intensity, reduce volume by 40-60%, and cut frequency by no more than 20%. Monitoring progress can be done using the rating of perceived exertion (RPE) with heart rate (HR) and power output. Nutrition during tapering should involve a reduction in energy intake to match the reduced training load, with the macronutrient composition remaining the same except for carb-loading in the final 48-72 hours. Extracted from the article
