AB  - Background: Diagnosing in sport is the process which is focused on the evaluation of athletes' preparedness in relation to the training load as much as other various factors, in order to control continuously the training process. Material and methods: Using the realisation of a selected swimming test for verification of the preparedness-level diagnostics is the basis of the experimental plan. Our experiment is based on the precondition that the 3000m crawl swimming test is considered to be a relevant indicator of the aerobic capacity preparadness level within the scope of swimming tests. During March and April 2005 61 swimmers aged 13-14 years and 44 swimmers aged 15-16 years were tested in cooperation with the National Sport Centre, Slovak Swimming Federation and swimming clubs, using the 3000m test. The date of testing had been planned for the end of the special preparation cycle of the summer macrocycle within the yearly training cycle 2004/2005. Capillary artery blood samples were taken in 3. and 10. minute after the swim. Results/Conclusions: After the evaluation of the findings gained from the blood lactate samples it is possible to state that the 3000m test is an appropriate tool for the evaluation of the aerobic capacity level and its changes during the specialized phase of a training programme in particular age group. The threshold pace, arrived at by the means of maximal effort of the 3000m, could represent the criterion of the swimming load intensity when repeating the sections under various load intensities. All the methods used for diagnosing have their strong and weak points. When these are implemented they provide coaches with quality and quantity of information which can only raise the informative value of the effectiveness of the training process. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
