AB  - Through literature review and logical analysis, this paper analyses the pacing strategy and speed features of world-class elite sprint cross-country skiers in multiple-round events, as well as the speed and technical features in single-round events, and discusses the key factors affecting the athletic performance of sprint cross-country skiers. The study holds that the physiological function system, neuromuscular system and body morphological characteristics are important factors that affect and determine the athletic performance of sprint cross-country skiers; among them, aerobic metabolic capacity, anaerobic metabolic capacity, work efficiency, lactic acid metabolism and recovery from fatigue are the key indicators affecting athletes' strategy for high-speed equilibrium pacing in multi-round events; upper limb explosive power, upper limb strength and endurance, muscle type, and neural coordination are the key indicators that affect athletes' choice of higher-grade sliding techniques, high sliding frequency and long sliding performance. At the same time, height, weight and lean weight, especially upper limb strength endurance, are the key indicators affecting the speed and endurance of athletes. Combined with the analysis of the models for annual and periodical training sessions for world-class elite sprint cross-country skiers, the study offers relevant suggestions on China's high-level sprint cross-country skiing training: 1) develop the aerobic metabolic capacity, and value low- and medium-intensity aerobic endurance training; strengthen anaerobic endurance training, especially the high-intensity training based on the intensity of glycolysis anaerobic energy supply, and improve the level of anaerobic endurance based on aerobic metabolism; (3) stress the training for gliding efficiency and motion economy, and strengthen specialized training for main sliding technical movements, technical efficiency and motion economy on specific terrains; 4) strengthen scientific training monitoring, prevent injury and fatigue, and actively prevent and control major public health crises to ensure uninterrupted high-level crosscountry skiing training. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
