AB  - Background: Studies and analyses of relationships between the applied loads and a sport result allow determining the influence of training on a reached sports level. Establishing real relations between the capacity and structure of the training loads and sports results is important for the whole training process. Material and methods: The purpose of the study was to determine changes in using training means from the third intensity level (aerobic-anaerobic effort) applied in marathon training by athletes with different sports competence. Records of realized training cycles by marathon runners with long training experience were used in the study. The most intensive training loads applied by the subjects in a year-training cycle were subject to analysis. Runners with the results below 2h 15min were selected for the first group, whereas for the second one those who had results between 2h 20min and 2h 25min. 35 annual training cycles in Group 1 and 40 in Group 2 have been analysed. The statistical analysis was based on data analysis software Statistica 5.0. Results/Conclusions: Athletes of higher sports competence apply more exercises in a training session and execute mixed effort from 8h to 23h 30min during a training cycle (15h 50min on average). In the second group these values reach from 2h 40min to 16h 18min, respectively (8h 46min on average). Different values for aerobic-anaerobic effort in group 1 and 2 have been established (test U; U=96.0; p<0.001). Marathoners whose results equal 2h 12min use from 2-3% (total loads) more of intensive means in training compared to less experienced runners (marathon results above 2h 20min). In such training runners cover a distance up to 400m by an interval method and a repetitive method is used in runs on a distance from 2 to 6m (5-12 times). ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
