AB  - At the initial phase of seasonal preparation, swimmers are submitted to a sudden increment of training load. Because the amount of training is normally less than the maximal that will be imposed later on in the season, coaches do not pay much attention to how athletes accommodate this increment. This study aims to analyse at rest, the variation of serum and salivary cortisol and testosterone, salivary IgA, HRV and the Profile of Mood States (POMS) after the first meso-cycle of a winter swimming season in a sample of 13 male Portuguese swimmers of national level. In this study, the impact of the initial phase of seasonal training on the athlete's adaptation in a multidimensional approach was investigated. Significant variation in hormonal, mood and autonomic variables were found, which justifies paying attention to athletes work tolerance, especially in the initial phase. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
