AB  - Background: The sports performance in 110-m hurdle race depends, among others, on the somatic build of runners and training types. The objective of this paper is to show differences in male hurdler training depending on the body build type. Material and methods: 53 Polish hurdlers (age: 21.81±2.05 years) representing the highest sport level (personal best: 14.15±0.51s) participated in this study. The best of them represented Poland during Olympic Games as well as during the World and European Championships in different age categories. Runners were divided into three groups according to the Rohrer index. The analysis of training programs contains 22 training means. Training loads included speed (1-3), endurance (4-7), strength-endurance (8-9), strength (10-16) and hurdle race technique exercises (17-22). The ANOVA analysis revealed differences of all variables (training means). Results: Research shows that body build type classification based on the Rohrer index determines volume and intensity of training. Each of three male hurdler types („thin", medium body build and strong body build) is characterised by specific training, when preparing for competition. Conclusion: The most significant differences were noted in special sprint exercises, tempo endurance, jumping exercises, dynamic strength and rhythm endurance. Statistical data show the specificity of the Polish male hurdler training in reference to their body build. Each of the three identified male hurdler somatic types is characterised by a specific training form based on the selection of particular exercise groups. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
