AB  - The article focuses on the differences in cardiac performance and pre-competition anxiety between half and full Ironman triathlon athletes. A study involving 15 participants revealed that half Ironman athletes spent significantly more time in higher heart rate zones compared to full Ironman athletes, who exhibited higher maximal heart rate variability during the race. Additionally, half Ironman athletes reported greater pre-competition somatic anxiety, which correlated with their time spent in higher intensity zones. The findings suggest that higher pre-competition anxiety may be linked to anticipated intensity rather than race volume, highlighting the need for further research on cardiac risks in ultra-endurance events. Extracted from the article
