AB  - Objective : To explore the effects of aerobic exercise on cardiac sympathetic nerve function and investigate the possible role of norepinephrine transporter (NET) in rats of heart failure. Methods : Thirty-eight Wistar rats were randomly divided into sham operation(Sham), heart failure sedentary (HF-sed) or heart failure exercise (HF-ex) groups. Rats of Sham and HF-sed groups kept sedentary while those of HF-ex group performed moderate intensity treadmill running exercise intervention for 10 weeks. After experiments, cardiac structure and function by ultrasound cardiogram, cardiac histopathological change by Masson staining, plasma and myocardial norepinephrine(NE) level by high pressure liquid chromatography, autonomic nervous function by frequency domain analysis of heart rate variability (HRV), cardiac sympathetic ganglion NET mRNA and protein, and myocardial total, membranous and cytoplasm NET protein expression by real-time Q-PCR and Western blot were measured. Results: Compared with Sham group, rats in HF-sed group presented pathological cardiac hypertrophy, myocardial fibrosis, sympathetic activity elevation(P< 0.05), myocardial and plasma NE increased CP <0.05), cardiac function reduced (P < 0.05); molecular biology assay discovered that cardiac sympathetic ganglion NET mRNA downregulated (P < 0.05), myocardial total and membranous NET protein reduced (P < 0.05) while cytoplasm NET protein upregulated CP < 0.05). Compared with HF-sed group, animals in HF-ex group showed conversion from pathological to physiological cardiac hypertrophy, reduced myocardial fibrosis, descended sympathetic activity (P < 0.05) and myocardial and plasma NE (P < 0.05), cardiac function enhanced {P < 0.05); cardiac sympathetic ganglion NET mRNA upregulated (P < 0.05), myocardial total and membranous NET protein increased (P < 0.05) while cytoplasm NET protein downregulated (P < 0.05). Conclusion: Aerobic exercise improved sympathetic overexcitation and delayed cardiac remodeling and heart failure by upregulating norepinephrine transporter gene expression and inhibiting its internalization. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
