AB  - The article focuses on the relationship between heart rate variability threshold (HRVT) and ventilatory threshold (VT) during cycling and running exercises. It describes a study involving eight healthy adults who underwent maximal graded exercise tests to assess how exercise modality affects peak oxygen consumption (V̇O2peak), maximum heart rate (HRmax), heart rate at VT, and the DFA-α1 metric at VT. The results indicated that cycling resulted in lower V̇O2peak and HRmax compared to running, and that HRVT overestimated the heart rate associated with VT during cycling. The authors suggest further research is needed to refine the DFA-α1 thresholds, as these may vary by exercise modality. Extracted from the article
