AB  - This article explains the key concept of supercompensation in sports training, which allows athletes to reach their peak condition, that is, their best physical shape on the day of the competition. The training load, defined by several factors such as intensity, duration, frequency, and density of exercises, must be sufficiently significant to provoke an appropriate stress, followed by adequate recovery, in order to promote performance progression without falling into overtraining. The article also addresses hypercompensation, a phenomenon where the sequence of several sessions with partial recovery leads to a greater improvement in physical capacities. Finally, it emphasizes the importance of planning training and recovery cycles, notably through weekly microcycles and recovery weeks, to optimize performance and avoid excessive fatigue. Extracted from the article
