AB  - The purpose of this study was to investigate if fitness status in female runners, based on maximal oxygen consumption, affects electrical activity of the myocardium as noted in electrocardiographic readings. A VO<sub>2</sub> max was performed on nine (n=9) elite female endurance runners (18 to 22 yrs of age) to obtain fitness status ranking (Moderate VO<sub>2</sub> max or High VO<sub>2</sub> max), while a standard 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) was performed for determination of sinus rhythm and resting hart rate (RHR). There was a significant difference (P=0.02) in VO<sub>2</sub> max values between the high VO<sub>2</sub> max group (59.7 ± 2.7 mL∙kg<sup>-1</sup>∙min<sup>-1</sup>) and the moderate VO<sub>2</sub> max group (54.2 ± 1.4 mL∙kg<sup>-1</sup>∙min<sup>-1</sup>). From the 12-lead ECG, we gathered that some changes due to heightened resting vagal tone and changes associated with physiological cardiac remodeling were present. There were negative correlations between sinus bradycardia and RHR (r = -0.81), sinus bradycardia and recovery hear rate (r = -0.71), MHR and increased QRS voltage (r = -0.75), and between RHR and early repolarization (r = -0.72). There was a positive correlation between VO<sub>2</sub> max and time to exhaustion TE (r = 0.86). It is important to recognize the difference between pathological and physiological adaptations when evaluating an athlete's ECG and the understanding that changes in myocardium may also be present in female athletes. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
