AB  - The article presents the Norwegian middle-distance training method, popularized notably by the Ingebrigtsen siblings and developed by middle-distance runner Marius Bakken. This approach is primarily based on a high volume of aerobic training at submaximal intensities, with specific work around the aerobic and anaerobic thresholds, including two weekly sessions known as "double threshold" workouts. Intended mainly for elite athletes capable of handling heavy training loads, this method requires rigorous monitoring of intensities through lactate measurements and cautious progression to avoid overtraining. It offers an interesting methodological alternative but is not universally applicable to all runners, particularly amateurs. Extracted from the article
