TY  - JOUR
AB  - An organism's anaerobic and aerobic capacity determines sportsmen's physical preparedness in stamina sports. It is efficient to apply physical loads for anaerobic stamina training when work intensity corresponds anaerobic metabolism threshold or exceeds it slightly. Anaerobic threshold limit may be changed performing training sessions. An anaerobic threshold limit changes while training and it is necessary to determine it every 1-2 months. This intensity limit is very significant in the training session process for choosing the training loads and training function abilities of an organism. The aim of the research - to investigate how an organism's adaptations of 16-18 year-old ski-racers change to progressively increasing run intensity indices during the preparatory macrocycle period, when the specification of training session changes. 16-18 years old ski-racers - the members (5 girls and 6 boys) of Youth Skiing Team of Poland were examined during the preparatory yearly macrocycle - in June, September and October. The training experience of the ski-racers was 5-7 years. Ski-racers have performed consistently increasing intensity run loads (on a stadium track) 6 x 6 min. After each run load a 2-minute rest followed and the blood test from the finger was taken in order to determine lactate. During the first run the heart systole's frequency (HR) was 50 impacts/min lower than maximal one. During each following run HR increased in 10 impacts/min. The last sixth time ski-racers ran using maximal efforts until they could keep the chosen run speed. After performing the whole run load the lactate concentration in the blood after 4 min, 8 min and 12 min was determined. While running, the oxygen uptake has been registered without breaking the run by a portative gas analyzer (K-4, Cosmed, Italy) and the heart systole's frequency - by a pulse meter "Polar" (Finland) The research results showed that VO2 of 16-18 year-old ski-racers increased as long as lactate concentration in the blood reached 8-9 mmol/l Further on, when the lactate concentration increases VO2 decreases. When VO2 decreases and the heart systole's frequency reaches 95-100 % of maximal one, ski-racers may keep up run load for 2-5 min. While reaching the anaerobic threshold VO2 of ski-racers reaches 47.34 +/- 2.97 ml/kg and it makes 81 % of VO2max. During the run test the heart systole's frequency reaches meanly 173 impacts/min (88 % HRmax) The heart systole's frequency of ski racers is 192 +/- 7 impacts/min while running at maximal efforts. The highest lactate concentration in the blood - 13.76 +/- 1.42 mmo1/1- was registered after run at maximal efforts and during 8 min of the recovery (restitution) period. The run speed of ski-racers while reaching anaerobic threshold is different and its change during the preparatory training session period determines the individual training methodics of ski-racers. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR]
