AB  - Purpose and Methods: Nine elite canoeists were investigated concerning changes in performance, heart rate variability (HRV), and blood-chemical parameters over a 6-d training camp. The training regimen consisted of cross-country skiing and strength training, in total 13.0 plus/minus 1.6 h, corresponding to a 50 % increase in training load. Results: Time to exhaustion (RunT) decreased from 19.1 plus/minus 1.0 to 18.0 plus/minus 1.2 min (P < 0.05). VO2max and max lactate (Lamax) both decreased significantly (P < 0.05) over the training period (4.99 plus/minus 0.97 to 4.74 plus/minus 0.98 L.min-1 and from 10.08 plus/minus 1.25 to 8.98 plus/minus 1.03 mmol.L-1 respectively). Heart rates (HR) decreased significantly at all workloads. Plasma volume increased by 7 plus/minus 7 % (P < 0.05). Resting cortisol, decreased from 677 plus/minus 244 to 492 plus/minus 222 nmol.L-1 (P < 0.05), whereas resting levels of adrenaline and noradrenaline remained unchanged. The change between tests in RunT correlated significantly with the change in HRmax (r = 0.79; P = 0.01). There were no group changes in high or low frequency HRV, neither at rest nor following tilt. Conclusions: The reduced maximal performance indicates a state of fatigue/overreaching and peripheral factors are suggested to limit performance even though HRmax and Lamax both were reduced. The reduced submaximal heart rates are probably a results of increased plasma volume. HRV in this group didn't seem to be affected by short-term overtraining.
