AB  - INTRODUCTION: The development of physical characteristics during a pre-season period is vital in preparing players for competition. Additionally, appropriate prescription of pre-season training loads will assist in targeting specific increases in fitness to maximise performance. However, the association between performance and various pre-season training activities is unclear. This study examines which pre-season training variables distinguish between higher and lower performing Australian footballers (AF) in the first four games of a season. METHODS: Data from forty-one elite male AF (mean±SD: age=23.4±3.1y; height=188.4±7.1cm; weight=86.7±7.9kg) from one Australian Football League (AFL) club were retrieved from the club's database. Pre-season training data (GPS, Rating of Perceived Exertion (RPE), fitness testing, session participation) was collected for 17 weeks (6 weeks pre-Christmas, 11 weeks post-Christmas). Total Champion Data ranking points (CDRP) and coaches' ratings (CR) (5-point scale; 1=significantly performed below AFL standard, 5=significantly performed above AFL standard) from the first four games of the 2016 AFL season were used as performance measures. A median-split based on performance measures was used to separate players into two groups (lower vs. higher). Independent samples t-tests were performed across all preseason training variables to examine group differences. Cohen's d effect sizes (ES) were also calculated. Meaningful relationships displayed statistical significance (p≤0.05) and an effect size (ES) of at least moderate (ES≥0.60). RESULTS: Pre-season training variables that displayed a meaningful relationship between the higher and lower performing groups are shown in Table 1. A strong positive correlation was also observed between CDRP and CR (r=0.970, p<0.001). DISCUSSION: Total session-RPE (sRPE) for on-field activities post-Christmas was significantly greater in the higher compared to lower group for both performance measures; suggesting that a greater on-field load after the Christmas break (likely through completing a high percentage of field sessions in combination with higher perceived physical load) could improve early season performance. This is supported by total time spent running (≥3m·s<sup>-1</sup>) post-Christmas, which is greater in both performance measures for the higher vs. lower group. Finally, this is reinforced by total sRPE for off-field activities post-Christmas in CDRP, which is significantly greater in the lower group. This could indicate that the lower group engages in more cross-training, rehabilitation or weight training, which potentially limits on-field training session participation and exposure to more competition specific preparation. PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS: Accumulating training load post-Christmas especially through on-field activities appears beneficial on early season performance. Finally, measures of internal load appear to be effective in predicting in-season match performance. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
