AB  - INTRODUCTION: Understanding the required training load to elicit improvements in intermittent running performance is a crucial process for strength and conditioning coaches. The aim was to investigate whether internal and external training load (TL) variables are related in a 'dose-response' manner with changes in 30-15 intermittent fitness test (30-15IFT) performance following a period of training. METHODS: Microtechnology data and session-rating-of-perceived-exertion (sRPE) were collected from 16 professional youth male rugby league players during a six-week pre-season period. The terminal running speed achieved during the 30-15IFT (v30-15IFT) prior to, and following the training period, represented a player's 'starting fitness' and 'end fitness' respectively. Derived from microtechnology, total-distance and PlayerLoad? were used to represent the 'global' external training load while sRPE represented the 'global' internal load. Total-distance was differentiated into the distance (m) covered at low- (SZ1; 0 to 3 m·s<sup>-1</sup>), moderate- (SZ2; 3.1 to 5 m·s<sup>-1</sup>), high- (SZ3; 5.1 to 7 m·s<sup>-1</sup>) and veryhigh- speeds (SZ4; > 7.1 m·s<sup>-1</sup>). The distance (m) covered above each player's individualised high-speed-threshold (> v30-15IFT [threshold range: 4.58 to 5.41 m·s<sup>-1</sup>]; IndSZ) was also determined. PlayerLoad was differentiated into low- (0 to 1 AU; PLZ1), moderate- (1.1 to 2 AU; PLZ2), high- (2.1 to 3 AU; PLZ3) and very-high (>3 AU; PLZ4) PlayerLoad?. A novel 'leave one out' partial least squares correlation analysis (PLSCA) was conducted to investigate which TL variables had the most influence on the shared information (inertia) with 'end fitness'. This involved creating a baseline PLSCA model with all TL variables and 'starting fitness' included and then systematically omitting each variable to understand the change in shared information and their subsequent variable importance. Variables deemed important were then included in a refined PLSCA and multiple linear regression model. RESULTS: The baseline PLSCA model was not significantly related with 'end fitness' (inertia: 18.76; P = 0.12). The findings of the PLSCA showed that a player's 'starting fitness' (change in inertia [CiI]: 4.14) and SZ4 (> 7 m·s<sup>-1</sup>; CiI: 3.61) distance (m) produced the greatest decrease in the measured inertia compared to baseline. This refined PLSCA model ('starting fitness', SZ3 and SZ4 distance) was significantly related (inertia: 17.54; P < 0.0001) with 30-15IFT 'end fitness'. Using multiple linear regression, this refined model explained a significant (P < 0.001) proportion of the variance (adjusted r2 = 0.73) in 30-15IFT 'end fitness'. DISCUSSION: The findings suggest that arbitrary 'high-intensity' speed-derived TL methods are related in a 'dose-response' manner that explain a large proportion of variability in the 'fitness' response while 'global', 'low-intensity' and 'individualised' TL methods (distance > v30-15IFT) do not. PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS: The accumulation of distance above 7 m·s<sup>-1</sup>, relative to a players starting fitness, appears important to understand changes in running performance following 6-weeks of training. Such analysis can be used to guide the prescription of load across a training period. For example, the refined regression model suggests that for a 1 km·hr-1 improvement in v30-15IFT following 6weeks of training, a player with a starting v30-15IFT of 18 km·hr<sup>-1</sup> should target an accumulation of 450m above 7 m·s<sup>-1</sup> compared to 800m for a player with a starting v3015IFT of 19.5 km·hr<sup>-1</sup>. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
