AB  - The effect of aerobic training on cardiovascular autonomic regulation was studied in 6 middle and long distance runners and 7 college students before and after 6 months long distance running training (20-25 km/week). Lower body negative pressure (LBNP) tolerance, heart rate and blood pressure responses to Valsalva manoeuvre, heart rate variability (HRV), systolic blood pressure variability (SBPV) and spontaneous baroreflex response slope (BRS) were measured. The results showed that aerobic training could induce decrease in LBNP tolerance, diastolic blood pressure recovery during phaseII and the maximum of heart rate of Valsalva manoeuvre, the total, low-and high-frequency powers of HRV spectrum, the mean BRS in the supine position, and the total and low- frequency powers of SBPV spectrum in the supine position, increase in LF/HF ratio of HRV spectrum during LBNP. It was suggested that aerobic training resulted in the decrease of cardiovascular autonomic regulatory function. The indices applied in this study can be used to monitor the effect of aerobic training on individual cardiovascular regulation.
