AB  - The article focuses on the study that examines the influence of training with low muscle glycogen in the fat metabolism, exercise performance, and skeletal muscle adaptation of well-trained cyclists. It states that the study was performed among 14 well-trained cyclists who were subjected to nine aerobic training (AT) and nine high-intensity interval training (HIT) sessions within three weeks. It reveals that training with low muscle glycogen decreased the training intensity of cyclists. It adds that the study indicates the rise in fat oxidation after training with low muscle glycogen. Moreover, training with high muscle glycogen did not increase triacylglycerol (TG) use.
