AB  - The article focuses on the investigation of heart rate variability (HRV) during isocaloric arm and leg cycling exercises at different intensities in males and females. The study involved 14 females and 10 males who completed trials of moderate and heavy intensity cycling until they burned 100 kcal. Results indicated a significant interaction between exercise mode and sex, with females showing higher RMSSD (a measure of HRV) during arm cycling compared to leg cycling, while no such difference was observed in males. Additionally, lower RMSSD values were recorded during heavy intensity exercise compared to moderate intensity, suggesting distinct autonomic nervous system responses based on sex and exercise mode. Extracted from the article
