AB  - Since the admission of triathlon to the Olympic Games in September 1994, the training of top-athletes is internationally orientated on the estimated winning time at the Olympic Games 2000. In short-distance triathlon (1,5 km swim, 40 km bike, 10 km run), the age of highest performance is known to be between 19 and 25 years, meaning that today's 16 to 21 years old athletes (juniors/youths) represent potential competitors for the 2000-Olympics. With these tendencies backgrounding, the training volume of 16 to 21 years old national class athletes (n=18) was documented (Season 1994, January to may 1995). In these athletes, preseasonal, incremental exercise tests on treadmill and bicycle ergometer were performed. In addition, the exercise stress during competition in a subgroup (n=12) was analysed (heart rate, lactate). The overall training volume shows wide spread of the individually realized training kilometres in the three disciplines. The training volume of the juniors was 2 to 3 times higher compared to the youth athletes. The demonstrated leg cycle and treadmill exercise tests are suitable to document the discipline-specific preseasonal evolution of the aerobic capacity. Significant correlations between the results of the exercise tests and the performance in competition can be shown. During a sprint (0,5 km swim, 19 km bike, 5 km run) and short distance triathlon (1,5 km swim, 38 km bike, 10 km run), average heart rates between 170 and 180 bpm and lactate concentrations of 7 to 10 mmo1/1 were measured. The data show that short distance triathletes perform constantly on exercise levels beyond their anaerobic threshold. In conclusion, in triathlon, in contrast with other endurance sports, the discipline-specific change of exercising muscle groups may allow a submaximal exercise level in competition approximately about two hours. However, the individual best times in the three disciplines, especially running, cannot be realized by the athletes in a triathlon competition until now.
