AB  - This investigation examined endurance runners during a 3-week reduction in training volume and frequency. Ten well-conditioned runners were monitored for 4 weeks while training at their normal weekly training distance (mean plus/minus SE) (81 plus/minus 5 km/week, 6 days/week). This period was designated as baseline training (BT). Sixty km/week were run at 75 percent VO2max, and the remainder (21 km/week) at 95 percent VO2max in the form of intervals and races. The runners then reduced weekly training volume (RT) by 70 percent of BT to 24 plus/minus 2 km/week and frequency by 17 percent to 5 days/week for 3 week. During RT 17 km/week was performed at 75 percent VO2max and the remainder (7 km/week) at 95 percent VO2max (intervals and races). The runners were tested weekly and performed 5-km races on a 200-m indoortrack during BT and after 2 and 3 weeks of RT. Maximal heart rate (HR) increased by 4 beats/min at RT week 3, which may have been associated with a decrease in estimated plasma volume of 5.62 plus/minus 0.43 percent. Time to exhaustion during the VO2max tests increased by 9.5 percent at RT week 3. No significant changes occurred with RT in body weight, percent body fat, overall 5 km race times, VO2max, muscular power (vertical leap and Margaria power test), and citrate synthase activity (at 2 weeks of RT). No alterations in venous lactate, energy expenditure, and HR were observed during submaximal running at two speeds (65 percent and 85 percent VO2max) with RT. It was concluded that reduced training maintained many endurance training adaptations and racing performance in these well-trained runners for 3 weeks.
