AB  - Traditionally, junior swim programs are characterized by high volume/ low to moderate intensity training which when coupled with high monotony may induce overtraining. In other training modalities high intensity interval training (HIIT) has been shown to improve performance. PURPOSE: to determine if high-intensity interval training (HIIT) in swimming had greater effects on performance and load characteristics than a load matched high-volume approach. The training program of junior swimmers (n=12, age 14.2±1.5yrs) was monitored and training load (distance x rating of perceived exertion) and training monotony were calculated over the course of 14weeks. The control phase (10weeks) was followed by a HIIT intervention phase (4weeks) which included 2 HIIT workouts and 1 recovery workout. Performance was measured using a 4x50 maximal anaerobic lactate test (MANLT) where blood lactate measured 3 min post exercise (BLA3), elapsed time (seconds(s)) and stroke rate (SR) were measured pre-observation (T1), post-observation/pre-HIIT intervention (T2) and post-HIIT intervention (T3). RESULTS: Mean training load was 7970±9441 and 5340±576 in the control and intervention phases, respectively. No significant difference between phases was seen in training load (p=0.44) or training monotony (p=0.49) indicating that the HIIT intervention did not impact training load variables. Whilst overall time of the MANLT showed no significant difference between T1(142.8±10.3s) and T2(144.9±8.7s) (p=0.14, r=0.90), BLA3 was significantly lower between T1(7.8±3.2mmol/L) and T2(5.6±2.2mmol/L) (p=0.01, r=0.57). In contrast, overall time of the MANLT from T2(144.9±8.7s) to T3(141.9±8.6s) demonstrated significant improvements (p=0.001, r=0.957) and BLA3 response following HIIT was also significantly higher (p=0.0004, r=0.81). CONCLUSION: These results demonstrate no performance change in the 10-week observation phase alongside a decrease in BLA3, a possible sign of overreaching. In contrast, a 4-week HIIT swim intervention demonstrated positive performance changes alongside an increase in BLA3 indicating a training adaptation to higher intensity, lower volume training in junior athletes. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
