AB  - Depressed baroreflex sensitivity (BRS) is known to be one of the risk factors in cardiac sudden death BRS, heart rate variability and hemodynamics were measured in 26 male runners (46.9±16.2 years old), before and after a half-marathon. BRS was attenuated from 17.5±9.9 to 9.6±6.5 msec/mmHg (P<0.01) LF/HF and LFnu: 55.9±17.3 to 61.8±14.6, p<0.01; HF: 914.7±1340.1 to 110.9±144.3 msec², p>0.01; HFnu: 44.1±17.3 to 38.2±14.6, p<0.01). The attenuation of BRS and HF was sustained for a period of 2.5 hours after the race. The heart rate (HR) increased and systolic blood pressure (SBP), diastolic blood pressure (DBP) and stroke index (SI) decreased after the race (HR: 62.9±7.9 to 79.7± to 79.7±10.0/min, p<0.01; SBP: 113.5±13.5 to 108.1±11.2 mmHg, p<0.01; DBP: 74.0±12.2 to 71.8±10.9 mmHg p<0.05, SI: 57.6±9.7 to 45.0±11.3 ml/m², p<0.01). The total peripheral resistance did not change. These data suggest that running a half-marathon attenuates BRS and exaggerates the balance of the autonomic nervous system after the race, and may induce myocardial depression. Great care must be taken during and after the race to prevent cardiac accidents associated with half-marathons. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
