AB  - The article presents a study which quantifies the reproducibility of responses to live high/train low (LHTL) altitude exposure in the same group of athletes. It explains that many athletes use altitude training to induce physiological adaptations similar to adaptations during improved performance. Subjects include 16 highly trained male and female middle-distance and distance runners who either underwent 2-3 week blocks of simulated LHTL altitude exposure or resided near sea-level as the control group. It reveals that there was a large individual variation in the change of both physiological and performance measures and concludes that competitive performance is not only dependent on physiological adaptations but on complex interactions between fitness, fatigue and training status.
