AB  - Purpose: We investigated the acute effects of cold water immersion (CWI) or passive recovery (PAS) on physiological responses during high-intensity interval training (HIIT). Methods: In a crossover design, 14 cyclists completed 2 HIIT sessions (HIIT1 and HIIT2) separated by 30 min. Between HIIT sessions, they stood in cold water (10 °C) up to their umbilicus, or at room temperature (27 °C) for 5 min. The natural logarithm of square-root of mean squared differences of successive R-R intervals (ln rMSSD) was assessed pre- and post-HIIT1 and HIIT2. Stroke volume (SV), cardiac output ( $$ \dot{Q} $$ ), O uptake ( $$ \dot{V} $$ O), total muscle hemoglobin ( t) and oxygenation of the vastus lateralis were recorded (using near infrared spectroscopy); heart rate, $$ \dot{Q} $$ , and $$ \dot{V} $$ O on-kinetics (i.e., mean response time, MRT), muscle de-oxygenation rate, and anaerobic contribution to exercise were calculated for HIIT1 and HIIT2. Results: ln rMSSD was likely higher [between-trial difference (90 % confidence interval) [+13.2 % (3.3; 24.0)] after CWI compared with PAS. CWI also likely increased SV [+5.9 % (−0.1; 12.1)], possibly increased $$ \dot{Q} $$ [+4.4 % (−1.0; 10.3)], possibly slowed $$ \dot{Q} $$ MRT [+18.3 % (−4.1; 46.0)], very likely slowed $$ \dot{V} $$ O MRT [+16.5 % (5.8; 28.4)], and likely increased the anaerobic contribution to exercise [+9.7 % (−1.7; 22.5)]. Conclusion: CWI between HIIT slowed $$ \dot{V} $$ O on-kinetics, leading to increased anaerobic contribution during HIIT2. This detrimental effect of CWI was likely related to peripheral adjustments, because the slowing of $$ \dot{V} $$ O on-kinetics was twofold greater than that of central delivery of O (i.e., $$ \dot{Q} $$ ). CWI has detrimental effects on high-intensity aerobic exercise performance that persist for ≥45 min. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
