AB  - The article focuses on the effects of aerobic training on cardiac autonomic function, specifically examining heart rate variability (HRV) and heart rate (HR) before and after graded exercise testing (GXT). A study involving 12 healthy young adults assessed physiological changes over 18 weeks of aerobic training, revealing that HR decreased and HRV measures, such as RMSSD and high frequency power, increased post-training. Results indicated significant differences in HR and HRV across various testing segments, with the lowest HR and highest HRV observed during resting periods. The findings suggest that aerobic training enhances cardiac autonomic function by reducing HR and improving HRV. Extracted from the article
