AB  - This study was designed to investigate the effects of pedaling rate on the cardiovascular, respiratory, metabolic, and perceptual responses of competitive road racing cyclists to a work load requiring 80% of their maximal oxygen uptake while riding their bicycles on a treadmill to simulate competitive cycling. The same responses were also measured during a series of trials with no external load being imposed on the cyclists to assess the effect of varying the rate of limb movements independent of external work rate. Expiratory flow oxygen consumption, blood lactate, net oxygen consumption, and net expiratory flow were quadrantly related to pedal frequency.
