AB  - The article presents a study which investigates the consequences of a 169-km ultramarathon to the cardiac function with the use of traditional echocardiography, speckle tracking imaging, cardiac biomakers, and heart rate variability (HRV). In the course of the study, the cardiac function of the subjects were examined before and after the ultramarathon. Left ventricular systolic and diastolic measurements were assessed with the use of traditional echocardiography. Meanwhile, the myocardial peak strain and strain rate were analyzed using speckle tracking. Results suggest that reductions in left ventricular function are not significantly associated with changes in cardiac biomakers and HRV.
