AB  - The effects of successive days of intense training on circulatory responses were investigated in male rowers. Fourteen national level rowers (18.6 +/- 2.0 years; 186.9 +/- 5.7 cm; 82.4 +/- 6.9 kg) were monitored over a six-day training camp. The training regimen consisted mainly of low-intensity on-water rowing and resistance training, in total 19.2 +/- 3.9 h, corresponding to an approximately 50 % increase in training load. 2000 metre rowing ergometer (Concept II, Morrisville, USA) performance time increased significantly from 395.9 +/- 10.8 to 404.2 +/- 11.9 s as a result of six-day training period. Mean power (from 361.9 +/- 28.5 to 349.0 +/- 32.8 W), blood lactate concentration measured five minutes after the test (from 19.2 +/- 2.9 to 16.2 +/- 2.3 mmol.l-1) and mean heart rate (from 184.6 +/- 7.5 to 179.2 +/- 7.4 beats.min-1) decreased significantly over the training period. Maximal oxygen consumption values remained unchanged (4.85 +/- 0.79 vs 4.83 +/- 0.61 l.min-1). The blood parameters of red blood cell count (from 4.910.2 to 4.7 +/- 0.2 x 102 1-1), hemoglobin concentration (from 14.9 +/- 0.6 to 14.310.6 gall-1) and hematocrit (from 43.4 +/- 1.7 to 42.3 +/- 1.8 %) were significantly decreased, while blood (+4.9 +/- 4.6 %) and plasma (+6.4 +/- 8.0 %) volumes were significantly increased after the training period. All measured blood parameters were not significantly correlated with the training volume (r < -0.44; p > 0.05). The change between tests in the mean heart rate demonstrated significant correlations to the changes in blood (r = -0.48) and plasma (r = -0.55) volumes. It was concluded that the reduced maximal rowing ergometer performance indicated a state of fatigue/ overreaching in rowers. However, comprehensive physiological testing was not sensitive enough to characterise dramatic increase in training volume of rowers.
