AB  - The effects of rapidly increased training volume on performance and recovery-stress state over a six-day training camp were investigated in highly trained male rowers (n = 17). The training regimen consisted mainly of low-intensity on-water rowing and resistance training, in total 19.6 +/- 3.8 h, corresponding to a 100% increase in training load. 2000 meter rowing ergometer performance time increased significantly from 396.9 +/- 10.8 to 406.2 +/- 11.9 s. The Recovery-Stress-Questionnaire for Athletes (RESTQ-Sport) revealed an increase in somatic components of stress (Fatigue, Somatic Complaints, Fitness/Injury) and a decrease in recovery factors (Success, Social Relaxation, Sleep Quality, Fitness/Being in Shape, Self-Efficacy). Significant relationships were observed between increased training volume, and Fatigue (r = 0.49), Somatic Complaints (r = 0.50) and Sleep Quality (r = -0.58) at the end of training period. Performance decrement indicated a state of short-term overreaching at the end of six-day heavy training period. Overreaching was further diagnosed by changes in specific stress and recovery scales of the RESTQ-Sport for athletes.
