AB  - The aim of this study was to determine changes in selected biochemical and hormonal parameters in runners during the 8-month training cycle within the preparatory training period, prior to the Hall and Summer Athletic World Championship in 1999. Six men - the elite members of the Polish Olympic team in the relay race 4 x 400 m. - participated in the investigation. In blood serum the following parameters were determined: urea and uric acid concentrations, total testosterone, cortisol and the creatine kinase (CK) and lactate-dehydrogenase (LDH) activity. After particular training cycles there were no changes in the urea and uric acid concentration or in the enzymes activity (CK, LDH). Decrease in the testosterone concentration and in the T/C ratio as well as increase in the cortisol concentration were caused by the training effort during a number of mesocycles led in the anaerobic-lactic acidogenous pathway. Mesocycle aerobic trials caused the cortisol-concentration decrease on one hand and increase in the testosterone concentration and the T/C ratio, on the other. The T/C ratio is suggested to be a particularly useful marker of the human organism response to stress induced by the training-load intensification. The findings prove that the endocrine system in the examined top-class competitors was significantly affected by a high-intensity exercise connected with the lactate-concentration increase in blood. Observed hormonal changes induced by the long-lasting anaerobic training may suggest decrease in the functional gonadal reserves.
