AB  - The aim of this study was to define the hormone excretion from pituitary - gonadal axis and suprarenal gland during the 5 months long training cycle in the preparatory period before the Olympic Games Sydney 2000. This research was carried out on 5 males - members of the Polish Olympic Team in the relay race 4 x 400 m. The determination was made in each of the successive five training mezocycles. The day before, the athletes avoided the physical effort. The blood samples were taken from the elbow vein at 8 a.m. immediately after the 15 min restitution in recumbent position. In the serum, the lutropine (LH), foliotropine (FSH), total testosterone (TT) and free testosterone (FT) concentrations as well as cortisol and dehydroepiandrosterone sulphate (DHEA-S) were determined. It was assumed that increase in the intensity and duration of the training loads is connected with the decrease in total and free testosterone as well as in cortisol After the periods of decreasing intensity of training loads these parameters increased. The gonadotropin and DHEA-S concentration changes were not unanimous. In the response to the same training stimulating impulse, some athletes revealed decrease and some increase in the same parameters. The estimation of the hormonal parameters should be made individually for the each competitor because of the possibility of individual response to the stimulating impulse. Generally, the decrease in level of such hormones as testosterone and cortisol should suggest the necessity of decreasing training loads, especially if change was comparative to the trainer's observations of the physical capacity.
