AB  - The purpose of this study was to determine the influence of physical training on cardiac parasympathetic tone and baroreflex control of heart rate (HR) and limb vascular resistance (VR) at rest in middle-aged and older men. 19 males (aged 45-68 yrs) participated in the study. HR variability was found to be greater after training. The magnitude of the reflex increase in forearm VR was reduced after training. The results showed that strenuous prolonged endurance training elicits increases in maximal exercise capacity and reductions in HR at rest; may increase cardiac vagal tone at rest; and does not change arterial baroreflex control of HR.
