AB  - 94 male junior rowers aged 16-20 years, 52 female junior rowers aged 16-19 years and 37 elite senior rowers (24 men and 13 women) aged 21-26 years performed a specific exercise on a rowing ergometer, simulating a rowing run at distance of 2000 m. The purpose of this work was to determine anaerobic threshold (AT) in young, inexperienced athletes of both sexes and compare them to senior elite rowers. Power at AT in younger juniors, older juniors and seniors was 226 plus/minus 36, 258 plus/minus 22 and 316 plus/minus 33 W, respectively - as regards men, and 153 plus/minus 22, 170 plus/minus 15, 212plus/minus 19 W - in relation to women. Heart rate (HR) at AT was about 87 per cent of HRmax in all groups. It was concluded that the performed exercise sufficiently well characterised the examined rowers in respect of the athletic experience so as to be used for the monitoring of metabolic adaptation and for calculation of training load intensity on the base of HR values at anaerobic threshold. The computed fiducial limits for AT index can be useful in a training progress evaluation in young rowers.
