TY  - JOUR
AB  - Development of endurance improves the adaptation of the organism. In order to ensure energy production at a required level, oxygen transport involves respiratory and circulation systems as well as different blood components. In sports, various forms of physical activity aimed at the development of speed endurance, as high intensity running, skiing, cycling, basketball, etc. are often used, which are based on anaerobic (without oxygen) energy production. The aim of the study was to establish changes in blood parameters in the case of physical loads directed at the development of speed endurance in athletes. The subjects were 15 cyclists aged 16-25 years. Physical loading represented cycling during which the subjects covered 25 km at maximum speed in the heart rate zone for the development of speed endurance. For analysis, rest venous blood was sampled one hour before the training, immediately after the training and at 2 and 18 hours of the recovery period after the training. Clinical analysis of the blood was performed using the blood analyzer Sysmex 2000. Hemoglobin (Hgb), hematocrit (Hct), erythrocyte (E) count, erythrocyte indices, leukocyte (L) count and leukocyte formula were determined. For assessment of the organism's adaptation to the physical load and for studying the specificity of the recovery period, 24-hour heart rate monitoring was carried out. For heart rate monitoring, sport testers Polar Accurex Plus were employed. The obtained results were processed with the Precision Performance Software. Statistical analysis of the results was performed using the program Statistica. The average heart rate of the subjects during the registered physical load was 176.0 +/- 6.46 (164-184) 1/m and maximum heart rate was 189.7 +/- 8.89 (173-203) 1/m. Speed during test cycling was uniformly high; fluctuations in heart rate were related to the track profile. This training load was directed to the development of speed endurance, which is evidenced by the high proportion (91.8 %) of the time spent in the heart rate zones of speed endurance. The results of the study demonstrate that changes in the average values of red blood parameters (Hgb, Hct, E), ensuring oxygen transport, in the post-load period have a small range and are unreliable (p > 0.05). Decrease in parameter values was noted immediately after the training; the decrease continued at 2 hours after the training, and recovery at 18 hours after the load was not complete. The indices of the studied erythrocytes (MCV, MCH, MCHC and RDW-CV) did not reveal a significant change in relation to the given physical load, either. Changes in the average values of the parameters of white blood in the post-load period showed a definite dynamics and had a wide range. Immediately after the physical load, leukocyte number increased and the increase continued at 2 hours after the loading. This increase in leukocyte number occurs at the expense of the increase in both the absolute and relative numbers of neutrophils. At the same time, there was noted a slight increase in the absolute number and a decrease in the relative number of lymphocytes and monocytes. Thus leukocytosis and changes in the proportions of white blood cells after physical loading show advancement at 2 hours after physical loading, and their recovery during 18 hours is not complete.
