AB  - The tolerable work duration (t) for high-intensity cycling is well described as a hyperbolic function of power (W): W=(W1.t-1) plus Wa, where Wa is the upper limit for sustainable power (lying between maximum W and the threshold for sustained blood (lactate) increase, theta lac), and W1 is a constant which defines the amount of work which can be performed greater than Wa. As training increases the tolerable duration of high-intensity cycling, we explored whether this reflected an alteration of Wa, W1 or both. Before and after a 7-week regiment of intense interval cycle-training by healthy males, we estimated theta lac and determined maximum O2 uptake (muVo2); Wa; W1; and the temporal profiles of pulmonary gas exchange, blood gas, acid-base and metabolic response to constant-load cycling at the above Wa. Although training increased theta lac (24 percent), mj Vo2 (15 percent) and Wa (15 percent), W1 was unaffected. For exercise at Wa, a steady state was attained for Vo2, (lactate) and pH both pre- and post-training, despite blood (norepinephrine) and (epinephrine) (NE), (EJ) and rectal temperature continuing to rise. For exercise greater than Wa, there was a progressive increase in Vo2 (resulting in mj Vo2 at fatigue), (lactate), (NE), (E) and rectal temperature, and a progressive decrease for pH. We conclude that the increased endurance capacity for high-intensity exercise following training reflects an increased W asymptote of the W - t relationship with no effect on its curvature; consequently, there is no appreciable change in the amount of work which can be performed above Wa. Furthermore, regardless of training status, Wa represents the upper power limit at which Vo2, blood (lactate) and blood pH can eventually be stabilized. Exercise greater than Wa, in contrast, is characterized by a steadily increasing Vo2 and blood (lactate), a falling blood pH and consequently, imminent fatigue.
