AB  - The purpose of this study was to investigate the effects of a mental warm down on the post workout recovery of high school swimmers. There were a total of 70 subjects (42 male and 28 female) from age 14 to 17, who participated in the experiment. The study lasted for 5 weeks. During the first week, a pretest was given to all subjects to determine the baseline for the mood states, anxiety level, and heart rates. During the following weeks, 25 minutes of breathing meditation were given to the experimental group after the physical warm down during the afternoon post workout session. The control group had 25 minutes of rest instead of breathing meditation. At the end of each week, the heart rate, mood states and anxiety tests were again administered to both groups. The findings of this investigation indicated that breathing meditation as a mental warm down method an facilitate heart rate recovery not only after the treatment (F=20.03, p<0.001), but also lasted until till the next morning (F=12.97, p<0.001). The experimental group also demonstrated a lower a global mood state (F=7.35, p<0.001), and decreased cognitive anxiety (F=12.33, p<0.001) compared to the control group. However, there was no significant difference in somatic anxiety, and some POMS sub-scales such as tension and vigor between the groups.
