AB  - This study aimed to examine the effects of aerobic training and detraining on heart rate variability (HRV), as well as the correlation between HRV, aerobic capacity and age. Five groups of male soccer players (adolescent, elite young adults, declining players (adult), active veterans and sedentary veterans) underwent spiroergometry and a heart rate variability analysis. Results showed that heart rate variability was dose-dependent on physical activity level, and that aerobic training produced increased HRV, independent of the subject's age. This study did not show that HRV index had a significant correlation to subject age.
