AB  - The aim of this experiment was to examine the effect of moderate treadmill exercise (TE) on the development of prostate cancer (PC) in rats, and also to find out if animals were homogeneous in their response to a given exercise load. 20 Lobund-Wistar rats were randomly assigned to the TE group and another 20 - to the control group (C). TE protocol consisted of 15-min running 3 times a week for 6 weeks. After that period, all animals were inoculated with transplantable rat PC cells - subcutaneously on the back above the sacral region (Day 0). The TE group exercised 2 more weeks after inoculation. V02max did not change in either group during the initial 6-week training period nor was different between groups at either time. The number of rats displaying tumour on Day 30 were 20 in the TE group and 18 in the C group. The total tumour volumes and numbers of lung metastatic lesions (LML), determined on Day 30 and 45 did not differ between groups. In the TE group, the post-training, maximum treadmill time and body mass were negatively correlated (r equal to -0.687; P less than 0.05) while training load and the number of LML - positively (r equal to 0.691; P less than 0.05). The TE protocol was not sufficient to elicit a training response and had no significant effect on the incidence or growth of PC in rats studied, tough the animals exercising at the highest intensity had the greatest number of LML. It appears that rodents may not be homogeneous in their response to a given load.
