TY  - JOUR
AB  - Practice as well as scientific studies have clearly shown the effect of fluid loss on physical performance capacity. Generally, a fluid loss of > 1 liter is known to influence oxygen transport capacity and physical performance. When exercise takes place in the heat, dehydration additionally reduces heat flux from the muscles to the periphery, causing the core temperature to rise which will have a further impact on central fatigue and performance. Additionally, thermal stress may increase to such an extent that normal organ functions and health become threatened. Both with respect to thermoregulation and performance, it is important for athletes to be maximally hydrated at the onset of exercise and to rehydrate appropriately during and after exercise. Studies in the field of gastric emptying and intestinal absorption have shown that well composed carbohydrate-electrolyte drinks are absorbed faster, retained better and improve performance more than plain water, mineral water and mineral water/apple juice mixtures. The need, however, to ingest a large amount of fluid is strongly influenced by the climatological conditions, as well as the type of exercise. As such, cyclists are able to ingest more fluid than runners. Generally, to hydrate rapidly and to maintain performance capacity at a high level, it is recommended that athletes ingest 400-800 ml per hour of a non hypertonic fluid which contains 60-80 g of carbohydrate and 400-1100 mg of sodium. The metabolic effects of endurance exercise have been studied extensively. One of the effects of physical exercise is that a large part of energy, which is transferred in substrate metabolism, is released as metabolic heat. Heat production will increase body core temperature which will signal the body to activate mechanisms to eliminate that heat in order to avoid severe hyperthermia. During intense exercise, the increase in body core temperature will rise 1 degree in every 5-7 minutes, if no cooling mechanisms would be activated. However, the increased blood flow induced by exercise will transport heat from the muscles to the periphery and once the temperature setpoint is reached sweat glands are activated to produce sweat. Each ml of sweat evaporating from skin will lead to a heat loss of about 2.5 KJ. Because sweat evaporation is a major way of heat elimination, a status of severe dehydration may occur with ongoing sweat loss, without fluid intake. This will impair blood flow as well as sweat production, resulting in hyperthermia by a reduction of heat flux from the body core to the periphery. The level of dehydration depends on quantitative sweat loss, which in turn is determined by exercise intensity and duration, by environmental conditions such as temperature and humidity, training status and type of clothing (3). In many sports events sweat rates may amount to about 1 liter per hour and exercising in the heat may increase sweat loss to more than 2 liters per hour. It has been found that exercise performance becomes impaired progressively when an individual is dehydrated by more than 1 % of body weight. Sweat losses of 2 % of body weight can reduce work capacity by 20 % (24). And when dehydration is greater than 5 % of body weight serious risks for the health such as heat stroke or heat collapse may occur, sometimes even resulting in death. It is therefore evident that an adequate fluid intake is necessary to avoid dehydration and harmful consequences for the health as well as to maintain performance during exercise.
