AB  - This review focuses on three essential questions: Does endurance training in older people improve aerobic capacity to a significant degree? Are the training adaptations in older people similar to those seen in young people? Is there a gradual loss of trainability with advancing age? More than 20 training studies are presented. Trainability in older people is quite well maintained and, when expressed in percent, comparable to younger people. With endurance training maximal oxygen uptake can be increased by 5-38 percent. Half of the "age-induced" loss in aerobic capacity, approximately one percent per annum, is actually due to inactivity. In addition, some of the limits of training studies in older subjects are illustrated.
