AB  - This study assessed the effects of external loading on power output in 10 children and 4 young adults during maximal vertical jumping. During unloaded conditions the absolute peak power output (W) achieved by children and adults was 572 W (45%) and 765 W (25%) respectively higher in cycling than jumping exercise. External loading reduced the generation of W immediately prior to take-off of a maximal jump from a force platform. This decrease in W with external loading is contrary to previous research.
