AB  - Electrophysiologic study (EPS) frequently is required to assess the prognosis of asymptomatic Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome (WPW) or to prove the nature of no documented tachycardia. EPS usually is performed by intracardiac route and hospitalization is required, but also can be performed by esophageal route during a consultation. The purpose of the study was to evaluate the cost of both techniques in France. Methods: Transesophageal EPS was performed in 100 patients with asymptomatic WPW syndrome and 100 with no heart disease, complaining of no documented tachycardias with abrupt beginning and end. The cost of transesophageal study including isoproterenol infusion is 127.75 Euros. The cost of intracardiac EPS is at least 1460 Euros, for 1 or 2 days of hospitalization. Results: In patients with WPW syndrome, 15 had a potentially malignant form and radiofrequency catheter ablation was indicated in a second time. In the group with no documented tachycardia, paroxysmal junctional reentrant tachycardia (PJRT) was induced in 30 patients and ablation was discussed. In 155 other patients with either a benign form of WPW syndrome or with a tachycardia unrelated to a PJRT, hospitalization was not required; intracardiac study performed during one day of hospitalization would have cost 226300 Euros. The cost for the esophageal EPS and a similar diagnosis was 19801 Euros, with a save money of 206499 Euros. In 45 patients in whom hospitalization was indicated in a second time to perform the arrhythmia ablation, the cost related to esophageal EPS was 5749 Euros. In the total group, considering the final diagnosis and the need of hospitalization in 45 patients, the save money related to the use of esophageal EPS was 206499 Euros – 5749 Euros = 200750 Euros. Conclusions: We should take into account the cost studies, when various techniques could be used for a similar diagnosis. There are important differences in the cost of diagnostic methods and it is easy to decrease this cost. Background: Alcohol (alc) withdrawal seizures and delirium tremens (DT) are serious complications of alc dependence. The prevalence of arrhythmias and other electrocardiographic (ECG) changes occurring in these clinical situations is not well studied. Methods: Led by the occurrence of a torsade de pointes in a patient (pat) with DT we performed an analysis of clinical data and of ECGs in pat discharged with the diagnosis of DT (ICD-Code F10.4) or alc withdrawal seizures (F10.3) within the last 10 years. All pat had an ECG written on the day DT or alc withdrawal seizures occured. The measurement of the ECG intervals was done in lead II. The corrected QT Interval (QTc) was obtained using Bazett's formula. The QTc was considered prolonged if it was longer than 440ms in males and longer than 460ms in females. Results: A total of 49 pat (38 males), mean age 48, were included in the study. 23 pat (49%) were admitted to the hospital with DT, 16 (34%) with convulsions and 10 pat (17%) developed DT while being hospitalized. The QTc interval was prolonged in 31 pat (63%). Laboratory parameters (sodium, potassium, creatinine) and intake of QT-prolonging drugs did not differ between pat with prolonged or normal QT intervals. 5 pat (10%), who all had a prolonged QTc, developed tachyarrhythmias: 2 torsade de pointes, 1 sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, 2 supraventricular tachycardia, 1 atrial fibrillation. All returned to sinus rhythm after appropriate treatment and no pat died. Conclusions: Tachyarrhythmias are common among pat with heavy alc withdrawal syndromes. The majority of the pat had an acquired long QT syndrome which led to torsade de pointes in 2 cases. Since laboratory parameters and intake of QT-prolonging drugs were similar in the two groups, we consider cerebral stress as one the factors for QT prolongation and proarrhythmic potential. QT-prolonging drugs should be avoided and the rhythm monitored in pat presenting with heavy alc withdrawal syndromes. Introduction: Presence of microvolt T wave alternans (MTWA), which reflects repolarisation alternans, is associated with an increased risk of primary ventricular fibrillation. In experimental studies, the highest dominant frequencies during ventricular fibrillation are found on the anterior LV wall. Methods and Results: MTWA assessment was done in 48 patients without residual myocardial ischemia, 8 to 12 weeks after acute myocardial infarction (AMI). All patients had left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) > 40%. MTWA was positive in 28% (n = 14), negative in 55% (n = 26), and indeterminate in 17% (n = 8) cases. Analysis of spatial distribution of alternans showed that precordial leads V2,V3,V4 presented the maximum level of sustained MTWA in 80% of cases with anterior infarction and in 70% of patients with inferior infarction (p = ns). Conclusion: In post AMI patients without LV systolic dysfunction, spatial distribution of MTWA is independent of initial localization of myocardial necrosis and affects primary the ECG projection of anterior LV wall. MTWA may be the result of primary left ventricular electrical remodeling. Introduction: It has been shown shown that repolarisation alternans can be the result of instability of intracellular calcium cycling. Method and Results: Exercise Microvolt T wave alternans (MTWA) was determined 8 to 12 weeks post AMI, in patients with normal LVEF. In 20 exercise MTWA positive patients, the level of alternans was further determined, during a standardized right atrial pacing protocol, in the basal status and after intravenous administration of Diltiazem and Verapamil. Thirty minutes after administration of Diltiazem the magnitude of MTWA was, compared to baseline: decreased in 50% of patients, increased in 30%, unchanged in 20% of cases. After Verapamil the results were: decrease in 60%, increase in 20%, no change in 20% patients. Conclusion: 1. There are different types of response of repolarisation alternans to CCB; this implies that electrical alternans must have more than one cellular mechanism. 2. Administration of calcium channel blockers (CCB) modified the magnitude of alternans in the majority of patients; CCB can influence the sensitivity of MTWA testing. Background: QT dispersion (QTd) represents a parameter of heterogeneity in the depolarization of myocardium of ventricles. The aim of this study was to examine the dynamic changes of QTd during the acute phase of myocardial infarction (AMI) as a non-invasive estimate of the success of thrombolytic therapy as well as its predictive value of the short term arrhythmic complications. Methods: Eighty patients with recent acute ST elevation MI were included in this study in addition to thirty normal (control subjects). Only 67 patients were treated with streptokinase due to contraindications. QTd (QTc max-QTc min) was measured manually immediately on admission (0 min), after the thrombolytic therapy (90 min), on the 2nd day and then before discharge (around the fifth day). Results: The eighty patients with AMI significantly increased corrected QTd (QTcd) as compared to the thirty control subjects 0.103 ± 0.026 versus 0.056 ± 0.072 (p < 0.001). After thrombolytic therapy, there was a significant reduction in QTd and QTcd dispersion as compared to patients who didn't receive thrombolytic therapy 0.073 ± 0.022 vs 0.097 ± 0.030 (p < 0.05). There was significantly less QTd in clinically successful thrombolysis (52 patients) than in failed cases (15 patients) (0.056 ± 0.017 vs 0.063 ± 0.012) (p < 0.05) and this difference was maintained till discharge. 15 patients (18.75%) experienced serious ventricular arrhythmias, However, we have found no significant difference in QTd parameters between patients with and patients without arrhythmias (QTd 0.085 + 0.021 vs 0.079 + 0.008, QTcd 0.103 + 0.027 vs 0.100 + 0.017)(p = N.S.). Conclusion: QTd is markedly increased in the acute phase of MI and shows significant reduction after thrombolytic therapy, especially the successful one. So QTd changes could be a new indicator of successful thrombolytic therapy but it seems not to be a valuable predictor of early post MI arrhythmic complications; however, this still needs larger studies. Purpose: To estimate R-R interval behavior using quantitative parameters of nonlinear dynamic methods and conventional heart rate variability (HRV) in patients (pts) with tachyarrhythmia recurrences after radiofrequency ablation (RFA) of typical atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT). Materials and Methods: Twenty-five typical AVNRT pts (6 women), 36.8 ± 9.2 years of age, were involved in the study. Arrhythmia history was 9.1 ± 7.7 years. Average rate of AVNRT paroxysms was 8.1 ± 7.7 per month. Successful RFA of AVNRT was performed. Quantitative characteristics of chaos we used as follows: parameters of informational dimension, fractal dimension (DF), and Lapunov's parameters. They were determined based on consecutive 4000 R-R intervals recordings before RFA, 6 hours, 24 hours, 2 months after RFA. Simultaneously time-domain and frequency-domain measurements of conventional HRV were analyzed according to short-term ECG recordings. Results: Recurrence of arrhythmia was observed during the period of 2 months of follow-up in 3 pts. No significant differences were found among standard HRV measurements as well as information dimension and Lapunov's parameter obtained before and after RFA in pts with AVNRT recurrences compared to arrhythmia recurrence-free pts. However AVNRT recurrence patients were expressed by significantly lower DF value 6 hours after RFA than those with no recurrences of arrhythmia (2.07 ± 0.03 vs. 2.60 ± 0.24, p < 0.05). Conclusions: Nonlinear dynamic methods might predict AVNRT recurrence in post-RFA pts. Conventional HRV is not informative in terms of arrhythmia recurrence rate prediction in pts underwent RFA of typical AVNRT. Recently we did show that VR (variability ratio) is a strong independent risk predictor for total and sudden cardiac death within 3 years from the index myocardial infarction when compared to other non-invasive risk markers. The purpose of this study was to evaluate if VR still after up to 7 years of follow-up was an independent risk predictor for total mortality. Methods: Twenty-four hour Holter recordings from 310 patients admitted with MI were performed 1-6 days after the MI. VR was expressed as the standard deviation of all QT intervals (SDQT) divided with the standard deviation of all RR intervals (SDNN). In the multivariate model we included: Age, diabetes, ventricular premature beats, ventricular tachyarrhythmias, bundle branch block, thrombolysis, beta blocker treatment, the mean RR interval, SDNN, SDQT, Ejection Fraction (EF), and VR. Results: During up to 7 years follow-up (mean 5.3 year) 96 patients died. In univariate analysis VR as a continuous parameter was significantly associated with mortality (p-value < 10-5) and performed best of all the examined risk predictors. The results of both forward and backward multivariate Cox analyses are shown in table 1. Conclusion: VR is an independent risk predictor for total mortality The aim of the study was to evaluate 24 hour ambulatory ECG measurements in patients with Chronic Heart Failure (CHF) and atrial fibrillation (AFIB) as a predictor of all-cause mortality. Methods: A 24 hour digital Holter was performed on 28 consecutive patients admitted with CHF and AFIB. QTC and heart rate (mean RR) were calculated as the mean of all QTC and RR intervals respectively. In the multivariate model we included mean RR, QTC, SDNN, SDQT (the standard deviation of all RR intervals and QT intervals respectively), the QRS width, age, and ejection fraction. Results: During follow-up (median 1191 days) 10 deaths occurred. In univariate analysis SDQT was significantly associated with all-cause mortality (p-value 0.009) and performed best of the examined risk predictors. In multivariate analysis both forward and backward selection resulted in a model containing only SDQT. Conclusion: SDQT predicted all cause mortality in patients with CHF and AFIB. This study shows that Holter parameters can be used in risk stratification of patients not only in sinus rhythm but also in AFIB. The aim of the study was to evaluate 24 hour ambulatory ECG measurements in patients with Chronic Heart Failure (CHF) as a predictor of all-cause mortality. Methods: A digital 24 hour digital Holter was performed on 65 consecutive patients admitted with CHF. Heart rate (mean RR) was calculated as the mean of all RR intervals. The variability of RR and QT intervals was expressed as the standard deviation of all RR and QT intervals respectively (SDNN and SDQT). Several parameters were tested univariate among others: EF, ventricular premature beats/hour, and QRS width. In the multivariate model only the best univariate risk predictors were included: mean RR, SDQT, SDNN, and age. Results: During follow-up (median 1228 days) 26 deaths occurred. The multivariate Cox analysis with backward selection and all-cause mortality as endpoint resulted in a model containing age and SDQT. Conclusion: Age and SDQT made the best model to predict all-cause mortality in patients with CHF. Aim: Several kinds of arrhythmias, especially ventricular arrhythmias and conduction disturbances, can occur during PCI. Supraventricular arrhythmias, including atrial flutter and atrial fibrillation, may also occur during or after PCI. The purpose of the study was to determine the clinical, angiographic, and electrophysiological predictors of AF after PCI. Methods: Two hundred twenty-five patients undergoing PCI (mean age 65 ± 11 years) and having sinus rhythm before balloon infilation were taken to study. The study population consisted of the patients with acute coronary syndromes. For all patients, ECG monitorization performed continuously during PCI. Twenty-two of 225 developed AF in catheterization laboratory after balloon infilation or in 24 hours following PCI (AF Group), 203 did not (SR Group). The P wave dispersion was calculated from surface ECG recorded before PCI. Results: The patients in AF Group were older (67 ± 9 years versus 63 ± 8 years), and their ventricular ejection fraction was lower than SR Group (56 ± 5% versus 45 ± 7%). In AF Group, the percentages of LAD and RCA interventions were 44%,11%, and 36% respectively. Those were 35%,15%, and 42% in SR Group. The P wave dispersion was significantly higher in AF group than in SR Group (53 ± 8 ms vs. 29 ± 10 ms, p < 0.001). In 16 of 80 patients with ACS/STEMI AF developed. For STEMI patients, the time from the onset of symptoms to balloon infilation was 3.7 ± 1.7 hours in SR Group vs. 4.1 ± 1.8 hours in AF Group, p < 0.05. Inferior MI was present in 5 patients (62%) in AF Group and 8 patients (30%) in SR Group. TIMI perfusion grades 2 and 3 were achieved in 23 of 27 patients in SR Group, and 5 of 8 patients in AF Group. Multi vessel disease was documented in 93 of the patients in SR Group vs. 12 in AF Group. Clinical reperfusion was thought to be established in 20 in SR Group, and 4 in AF Group. Conclusions: Despite some limitations, our results show the importance of clinical factors, angiographic results and P wave analysis in prediction of AF following PCI. Purpose: The intracardiac farfield electrocardiogram (EGM) of implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICDs) provides data of cardiac ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. Diagnostic capabilities of ICDs may be improved by QT measurements. We therefore examined the relationship of QT duration in the EGM and standard surface ECG and its reproducibility and stability during activity. Methods: In 27 patients with single-chamber ICDs, shock EGM and standard 12-lead ECG were simultaneously recorded and displayed on a multichannel EP system. Measurements were performed in basal heart rhythm and during right ventricular pacing with 60, 80, 100, and 120 bpm. For statistical comparison of QT duration in EGM and any of the 12 standard ECG leads, a Bland-Altman analysis was chosen. In the next step, EGM and surface ECG lead I were simultaneously recorded using special software with the patient supine, upright, and during Valsalva's and knee-bends maneuvers. Results: QT duration was systematically longer in the EGM as compared to ECG during basal rhythm (439 ± 38 ms vs. 424 ± 40 ms, mean + 3.5%) and ventricular stimulation (mean + 4.2%). QT-interval in EGM and ECG correlated well (r = 0.86) with the best correspondence between EGM and V4 (mean difference 19 ms, range −21 to 59 ms of measured pairs). During different positions and maneuvers, the EGM was more stable and much less affected by moving artefacts. Conclusions: Ventricular EGM duration provided by ICDs is systematically longer and correlates well with QT duration in the standard surface ECG, especially lead V4. Due to its stability during activity, the implementation of EGM measurement in ICDs may be feasible and valuable. Especially in the field or Home Monitoring, its serial registration may be used to monitor the effect of QT prolonging drugs and/or to predict and anticipate arrhythmias preceded by repolarisation changes. Background: Abnormal electrocardiographic changes of ventricular repolarisation can be quantitatively evaluated by T wave morphology (TWM), which may reflect clinical myocardial damage. Some TWM descriptors have been demonstrated to be strong and independent prognostic stratifier in post myocardial infarction (MI) and in general population. This study investigated the association between TWM and myocardial damage and/or arrhythmic complications in patients (pts) with angiographically documented coronary artery disease (CAD). Methods: We studied (i) 35 CAD pts (MIVT; age 64 ± 8 years, 30 men) with documented history of remote MI and a history of ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation and 35 CAD pts without such history who were pair-matched for age and gender with MIVT group, (ii) 16 CAD pts with acute coronary syndrome (ACS; age 67 ± 7 years, 13 men), and 16 CAD pts without MI and arrhythmic history pair-matched for age and gender. Four TWM descriptors were calculated from standard 12-lead ECGs using in-house programme by an operator blinded to clinical data: TWM dispersion (TMD), which was also calculated separately in ascending (aTMD) and descending parts of the T wave (dTMD), and 3-D QRS-T angle (TCRT). Results: Both MIVT and ACS showed significantly increased TMD (57.6 ± 4.1 v 21.4 ± 3.7, p = 0.000; 41.7 ± 8.0 v 19.9 ± 4.3, p = 0.039, respectively), aTMD (59.1 ± 5.1 v 23.1 ± 4.3, p = 0.000; 39.8 ± 9.1 v 19.1 ± 5.3, p = 0.030), and dTMD (58.8 ± 4.4 v 15.7 ± 3.2, p = 0.000; 40.3 ± 8.1 v 16.1 ± 4.1, p = 0.034) while TCRT was significantly lower in MIVT (-0.055 ± 0.118 v 0.456 ± 0.085, p = 0.006) or ACS (-0.193 ± 0.121 v 0.271 ± 0.127, p = 0.044). No significant difference was noted in the number of diseased coronary arteries between groups. Conclusion: TWM abnormalities are notably associated with myocardial damage and arrhythmic complications in CAD. Incorporation of these TWM quantifiers into risk assessment algorithm may improve the efficiency of risk stratification of pts with CAD. Ventricular gradient (VG) is a descriptor of repolarisation heterogeneity, which quantifies the deviation between the directions of ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. The value of VG as a risk stratifier in post-infarction (MI) patients has already been demonstrated. In this study, we investigated the diurnal variation of VG in post-MI patients. Methods: During a 24-h recording (SEER MC, GE Marquette) in 117 consecutive MI patients before hospital discharge (age 64 ± 1 years, 76 males), a 10-s 12-lead ECG was obtained every 10 s. For each ECG, QT and QTc (Fridericia) intervals were obtained automatically and VG [°] was computed from the automatically calculated QRS and T wave areas in derived X, Y, and Z leads (ECG Research Workstation, GE Marquette). Median values of heart rate (HR), QT, QTc, and VG were compared between daytime (08.00 to 11.00h) and nighttime (00.00 to 03.00h). Results: Sixty-three (54%) MI patients received thrombolysis, whereas 39 (33%) suffered from anterior MI. HR was higher (p < 0.001) while QT (p < 0.001) and QTc (p < 0.001) were lower during the day compared to the night in thrombolysed as well as in non-thrombolysed patients. VG was higher during the day (91 ± 5°) compared to the night (86 ± 6°, p = 0.09) in thrombolysed as well as in non-thrombolysed patients (94 ± 7° vs 87 ± 7°, p = 0.025). HR and QT interval day-/nighttime differences were preserved in patients with both anterior and inferior MI. VG was higher during the day (89 ± 6°) compared to the night (83 ± 6°, p = 0.005) in patients with inferior MI, whereas it did not differ between respective time periods in patients with anterior MI (102 ± 7° vs 103 ± 8°, p = 0.52). Conclusion: In post-MI patients, VG exhibits diurnal variation mainly related to the infarct location and not to the administration of thrombolytic therapy. Preserved diurnal variation of VG in patients with inferior MI may indicate better adaptation of repolarisation, which may be related to better prognosis. Introduction: Ventricular arrhythmias with left bundle branch block morphology in apparently healthy people usually arise from the right ventricule. The differentiation between right ventricular outflow tract tachycardia (RVOT) and arrhytmogenic right ventricular dysplasia (ARVD) is important clinically when discussing prognosis and management options in this patients, and it is more difficult at the initial stage of the disease. Objective: The aim of this study was to compare the two conditions with regard to the electrocardiographic characteristics in patients with ARVD or RVOT tachycardia. Methods: We study 37 consecutive patients with right ventricular tachycardia and apparently without significant structural heart disease: 9 with ARVD confirmed with the European Society of Cardiology criteria and 28 with RVOT with exclusion diagnosis. Results: QRS V1 >110 msc N (%) ARVD 7 (77), RVOT 3 (11) p < 0.01; V1-3/V4-6 N (%) ARVD7 (77), RVOT 1(3.5) p < 0.01; Precordial negative T wave N (%) ARVD 7 (75), RVOT 3 (11) p < 0.01; RBBB N (%) 1 (11), RVOT 1 (3.5) ns; Epsilon wave N (%) ARVD 2 (22), RVOT 0 p0.05. Inferior frontal VT axis N (%): ARVD 6(66.5), RVOT 28 (100) ns; >1 VT morphology N (%) ARVD 5 (55), RVOT 2 (7) p0.01. In multivariable analysis a V1>110 msec QRS had a positive predictive value of 85% and a negative predictive value of 90%. Conclusions: The electrocardiographic features are very useful in the differential diagnosis of ventricular tachycardias arising in the right ventricule. Alterations in despolarization/repolarization had a high positive and negative predictive value for the diagnosis of ARVD. We studied 3 patients (1 female), mean age 35 ± 2.52 years, with symptomatic idiopathic LBBB-configuration ventricular premature beats (VPB) originating from right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT). Pts underwent electrophysiological study and radiofrequency catheter ablation (RFA) of VPB using traditional mapping technique (activation mapping and pace-mapping). Antiarrhythmic drugs were discontinued for at least 5 half-lives before operation. We registered sharp potential preceding the ventricular premature complex by 21.3 ± 5.5ms and late depolarization during sinus rhythm in site of ablation in all cases. RFA was performed with a preset temperature of 60 degrees and power limit of 50 W. Ventricular fibrillation (VF) was induced immediately after ablation started. Average delivering time VF was 15 ± 4 seconds effectively terminated by external defibrillation in 29 ± 5.5 seconds after manifestation. During 13.4 ± 1.3 mos. of follow-up period 2 pts remained free of VPB recurrence while the other patient was out of clinical post-RF efficiency. Conclusion: RF-delivering while guided by sharp-potential mapping illustrating so-called fascicular activation within RVOT and late depolarization in the RVOT may initiate VF. These findings corresponds to dominant triggers from distal Purkinje system initially described by Haissaguerre but located in RVOT. Background: Recently, it has been shown that left ventricular function (LVF) can improve dramatically after ablation of symptomatic frequent premature ventricular complexes (PVCs) originating from right ventricular outflow tract (RVOT). The incidence of LV dysfunction among patients with frequent PVCs is unknown. It is the objective of this study to evaluate the prevalence of LV dysfunction among the patients with frequent RVOT-PVCs on ambulatory monitor. Methods: We reviewed the Holter monitor performed between 1/1/2001 and 6/30/2005. Frequent PVCs was defined as PVCs more than 10 per hour. PVC originating from RVOT was defined as PVC with left bundle branch block morphology with inferior axis on 12-leads. Patients were divided to three groups based on the frequency of PVCs; less than 1000/day, 1000-10000/day, and more than 10000/day. LV dysfunction was defined as LVEF less than 55%. Results: Among 500 patients who had frequent PVCs, we identified 169 patients with PVCs mainly from RVOT. 110 patients with echocardiography results were included in the analysis. Mean age was 53 +/− 16.2, and 34/110 (31%) were male. 30 patients had PVCs less than 1000/day, 50 patients had PVCs between 1000-10000/day, and 30 patients had PVCs more than 10000/day. 21 (27%) patients had EF less than 55%: 2/30 (6.7%), 10/50 (20%), 9/30 (30%) in each group (p = 0.069). Conclusion: These results indicate that there is a significant association of LV dysfunction and frequent RVOT-PVCs. 30% of the patients with RVOT-PVCs more than 10000/(day have LV dysfunction. Based on the recent studies, these patients may improve their LV function with treatment of PVCs, and thus ablation may be a reasonable treatment option for those patients. Ventricular arrhythmias are rare and represent the most serious arrhythmic complication after coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG). The present study was conducted for identifying patients at risk of ventricular arrhythmias with ventricular signal averaged ECG, standard deviation of all normal RR intervals (SDNN), angiographic and echocardiographic data. We defined ventricular arrhythmias as ventricular fibrillation and ventricular tachycardia. The study population consisted of 209 consecutive patients with sinus rhythm undergoing CABG. The primary endpoint was the occurrence of VA after CABG. The secondary endpoints were hospital length of stay after CABG and the occurrence of VA after hospital discharge. During the postoperative follow-up ventricular arrhythmias were observed in 11 patients (5%). Patients with ventricular arrhythmias showed a higher incidence of ventricular late potentials (91 vs. 9% of patients, p < 0.0001) than patients without ventricular arrhythmias. In addition patients with ventricular arrhythmias had a lower left ventricular ejection fraction (44.2 plusminus 15.2 vs. 60.1 plusminus 13.1%, p < 0.0001) and a SDNN (22.4 plusminus 8.8 vs. 34.4 plusminus 16.1ms, p < 0.02). A stepwise logistic regression analysis of all variables identified the combination of ventricular late potentials, ejection fraction < or equal38%, and SDNN < or equal28 ms (odds rate 26.00; 95% CI, 3.44-196.67, p < 0.002) as an independent predictor of ventricular arrhythmias. The results of our study suggest that the probability of ventricular arrhythmias could be predicted after CABG by a combination of low left ventricular ejection fraction and a measurement of ventricular signal averaged ECG and standard deviation of all normal RR intervals. Patients who can be identified as having a high risk of ventricular arrhythmias should be observed carefully after surgery. Introduction: Acute, infective myocarditis (IM) has been claimed as one cause of sudden death (SD) in athletes. Thus, a protocol including non-invasive cardiology tests (NICT) and laboratoristic profile (LP) has been set-up. Patients and Methods: 119 young subjects practicing sport activities (SA) were selected for frequent ventricular arrhythmias (VA), worsening of pre-existing VA, with or without evolutive ST T changes. They received NICT and a LP, including IgM, IgA, and IgG versus the most common viral and bacterial infections, which was repeated every 3 months, till normalization. Results: Fifty-six of the 119 subjects (47%) showed LP compatible with an immunologic state versus specific infections. 28 of the 56 subjects with positive LP had also clinical signs of an acute systemic illness (SI) with a concomitant myocardial involvement (MI). In 26 of 28, SA has been discontinued and resumed in 20, after 6-9 months, after normalization of the LP. In 28 with LP, 12 cases were due to Echo Coksackie B Enterovirus; 4 cases to Toxoplasmosis, of which 1 was lethal and 1 evolved into dilated cardiomyopathy; 4 cases to infectious mononucleosis; 2 cases to flu virus or Adenovirus, 2 cases to Mycoplasma Pneumoniae; 2 cases to Herpes Virus or Herpes Zoster infections; 1 case to borelliosis or Lyme's syndrome; and 1 case to Legionellosis. Conclusions: The signs of a typical SI could be easily identified in some athletes (47%). Clinical data and the clinical picture of an upper respiratory tract or low bowel infection, in association with a typical LP, can be regarded as highly indicative of a SI. A concomitant clinical picture, including signs of pericarditis and or ST changes, frequent VA, or worsening of VA can be considered highly indicative of MI involvement during a SI. SA should be discontinued for 6 months or more and resumed after normalization of NICT and LP. Introduction: The prognostic significance of family distribution of cardiac arrest (CA) or sudden death (SD) in Brugada syndrome patients is still debated. We verified the importance of the ECG aspect (coved versus saddle back type) in association with family distribution (FD) and the extent of FD of CA and SD in our families of BS patients. Patients and Method: We evaluated in our thirteen families the ECG pattern (spontaneously or drug induced coved type), the FD, and the extent of FD of CA and SD, in relation to new episodes of CA or appropriate ICD shocks (AIS) in a 9 ± 5 years cumulative follow up. Results: The coved ECG type was documented in 8/13 families; 6/8 had new CA or AIS, while other patterns were documented in 5/13 in whom only a new CA was documented FD was observed in 7/13 and new CA or AIS were observed in 6/7 families. Considering the extent of FD, more than 4 episodes of CA and SD were observed in 4/13 families associated with 8 new episodes in all four. Conversely, 2-4 episodes were observed in 3/13 families associated with 2 new episodes of CA or SD. In the third group, less than 2 episodes in the FD were observed in 6/13, associated with no new episodes. Conclusion: Further evidence has been provided in our patients population suffering from BS, showing that the coved type (spontaneously occurring or after drug test), FD, and the extent of FD of more than 4 episodes of CA or SD are strongly associated with a poorer prognosis or more frequent AIS. Background: Electrical stun guns are being increasingly used by law enforcement authorities in their field operations. There are reports of deaths associated with their use, often in violent subjects who were found to be intoxicated with cocaine, amphetamines, and other drugs of abuse. We tested if cocaine intoxication would increase the vulnerability for ventricular fibrillation with stun guns. Methods: We tested 5 adult pigs using a custom device built to deliver multiples of standard shock that matched the waveform of a commercially available electrical stun gun (TASER® X-26, TASER International, Scottsdale, AZ, USA). Shocks were applied in a step-up and step-down fashion, using two tethered darts at five locations: Sternal notch to cardiac apex (position-A), sternal notch to supra-umbilical (position-B), sternal notch to infra-umbilical (position-C), side to side on chest (position-D), and upper to lower mid posterior torso (position-E). VF threshold (VFT) was determined by averaging the maximum safety multiple (highest strength not inducing VF) and minimum VF inducing multiple (highest strength inducing VF), before and after cocaine infusion. Findings: Standard shocks (x1) did not cause VF at any of the 5 locations before or after cocaine infusion. The VFT of electrical stun guns progressively increased from position-A though position-E before and after cocaine infusion. There was a 1.5 to 2.5-fold increase in these values at each of the five tested positions after cocaine infusion, suggesting decreased cardiac vulnerability for ventricular fibrillation to NMI discharge. No significant changes were seen in CK-MB and Troponin-I before and after cocaine infusion with NMI discharge applications at multiple strengths. Also no significant metabolic and hemodynamic changes were seen. Interpretation: Cocaine increased the VFT of stun gun shocks in all dart locations tested and reduced cardiac vulnerability to ventricular fibrillation. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
