AB  - The article focuses on the monitoring of training load and recovery in elite athletes, specifically examining a Canadian rowing athlete's performance and psychophysiological responses over a 6-week training block. The study involved a 20-year-old male athlete who underwent performance assessments and daily surveys to track various metrics, including sleep, perceived exertion, heart rate, and training volume. Results indicated no significant improvements in performance metrics from week 1 to week 6, despite the athlete reporting adequate sleep and low stress levels. The authors suggest that future research should explore interventions aimed at enhancing performance through effective training and recovery strategies for recreational athletes. Extracted from the article
