AB  - Elite male and female athletes (N=330) were isokinetically screened for peak torque generating capabilities and muscle imbalances. The male athletes included 45 sprinters (more than or equal to 400 m), 56 middle distance runners (more than or equal to 3,000 m), 50 long distance runners (less than 3,000 m), 48 jumpers and 19 throwers. The female athletes represented 44 sprinters (less than or equal to 400 m), 24 middle distance runners (less than or equal to 3,000 m), 17 long distance runners (less than 3,000 m), 13 jumpers and 14 throwers. A Cybex II isokinetic dynamometer was used to measure dominant leg strength and quadriceps/hamstring ratios at 30 degrees per second. The MANOVA procedure revealed significant differences between groups for male and female absolute extension and flexion (Nm). However, when strength was expressed relative to body weight (Nm/kg) no comparisons were significant. Given that absolute strength scores correlated with body weight, peak torque/body weight may be more sensitive than absolute values as indicators of underlying factors (such as fibre type composition or training status) responsible for the differences in muscular strength between groups. The study helped establish normative data on quadriceps/hamstring ratios on athletes. Furthermore, the ratio provides a guide to observe muscle imbalances which could be a factor in injury.
