AB  - The article focuses on identifying gait metrics measured by consumer-grade wearable sensors that are associated with internal forces during running, which could help predict running injuries. Six runners participated in a treadmill study where they wore chest and shoe sensors while running at varying speeds. The study found that maximum forces on the Achilles and patellar tendons increased with speed, while decreased cadence and increased vertical oscillation were linked to greater internal tendon forces. The results suggest that these gait metrics could be useful for monitoring training loads in real-world settings. Extracted from the article
