AB  - The article focuses on the relationship between postexercise hypotension (PEH) and heart rate variability (HRV) in adults with hypertension following an aerobic training program. The study involved middle-aged participants who engaged in a 12-week moderate to vigorous intensity aerobic training regimen, with measurements taken before and after the training to assess changes in blood pressure and HRV. Results indicated that higher levels of HRV, particularly the standard deviation of NN intervals (SDNN) and high frequency HRV, were associated with greater reductions in blood pressure after exercise, suggesting that resting autonomic activity plays a significant role in PEH. The findings emphasize the need for further exploration of autonomic function in relation to exercise-induced blood pressure changes. Extracted from the article
