AB  - This article presents new briefs related swimming and human health, as of October 1, 2005. A study conducted at the University of Colorado found that "apple-shaped" women, who store fat in their waists and torsos rather than thighs and lower midsections, run an increased risk of heart disease and generally have higher cholesterol levels than women with other body types. Another study at Emory University School of Medicine has found that men and women over 60 who take daily fish oil supplements can improve the health of their heart, measured by heart rate variability, in as little as two weeks.
