AB  - Our purpose was to compare bone mineral density (BMD) and hormonal status of adolescent female competitive swimmers, gymnasts, and normally active non-athletes. Specifically, we assessed BMD of the hip (femoral neck, Ward's triangle, trochanter), and lumbar spine (L2-L4, lateral view) of competitive gymnasts (G, N=10) who were matched for age (11.2 plus/minus 0.4 yr, mean plus/minus SE, range 9-14 yr) and training status in terms of duration, frequency, and relative intensity. Dependent variables were examined by ANOVA followed by Tukey's post hoc tests. The gymnasts were significantly shorter, lighter in weight, and had lower percentage of body fat compared with both swimmers and controls. No significant differences in lean tissue mass (kg), excluding bone mass, were noted between groups. Maturational age, assessed by Tanner stage (I-IV), was nonsignificantly lower in G(2.1 plus/minus 0.35) compared with S(2.7 plus/minus 0.34) and C(2.8 plus/minus 0.44). No differences in height, weight, percent fat, or Tanner stages were noted between S and C. Likewise, serum estradiol (pg/mL) was nonsignificantly lower (p less than 0.39) in G (63.0 plus/minus 20.5) compared with S(106.1 plus/minus 30.3) and C (106.1 plus/minus 23.8). Insulinlike growth factor I (IGF-1) was significantly lower in G compared with C(p less than 0.006). Bone mineral density (g/cm2) was consistently highest in G and lowest in S at all sites, although significantly different between G and S only at Ward's triangle and the trochanter sites. Thus, although G were lower in body weight and body fatness and were slightly less mature than S or C, they had the highest bone density at all sites measured. These data indicate that the high-impact activity of gymnastics is beneficial for bone health of periadolescent girls.
