AB  - The article investigates the impact of training status on electrocardiographic (ECG) findings among individuals with varying exercise backgrounds, specifically untrained (UT), endurance-trained (ET), weight-trained (WT), and those who are both endurance-trained and weight-trained (EWT). A total of 104 subjects were analyzed through 12 lead ECG recordings, revealing significant differences in heart rate (HR), QT interval duration, and QRS axis primarily between the UT and ET groups. The study found that endurance training was associated with lower HR and longer QT intervals, likely due to increased parasympathetic activity and cardiac adaptations. Additionally, the prevalence of certain ECG abnormalities was notably higher in the ET group compared to the others. Extracted from the article
