AB  - The article investigates the differences in muscle oxidative capacity and muscle diffusing capacity (DmO2) between sexes in endurance-trained athletes, focusing on the vastus lateralis (VL) and medial gastrocnemius (GS) muscles. Using near infra-red spectroscopy (NIRS), the study found that muscle oxidative capacity, indicated by the recovery rate constant (k), was lower in females compared to males and also lower in the VL than in the GS. Additionally, while there was a trend suggesting greater capillary-to-fiber ratio (C:F) estimates in females, no significant interactions between muscle type and sex were observed. The findings indicate potential differences in training adaptations related to muscle oxidative capacity and DmO2 between male and female athletes. Extracted from the article
