TY  - JOUR
AB  - Flatwater kayaking and waterpolo according to their respective training load and training exercise structure are basically aerobic sports. Considering the above-mentioned, body composition and spiroergometric data of the two sport branch specific sample should not have significant differences in their maximal values. Up until now no comparative data is available in the literature about world class waterpolo players and kayakers concerning their cardiopulmonary system and body composition. The purpose of this study was to measure the physiological capacity of the cardiopulmonary system kayakers and waterpolo players selected from the Hungarian National Team. It was, also, aimed to compare the anthropometric and spiroergometric data of waterpolo players and kayakers. We assumed that despite of the two sport events require high level aerobic capacity, the different, sport specific requirements and training influence the same parameters differently. Nine waterpolo players (G2) members of the World Cup winning team - the goal keeper excluded- and nine kayakers (G1) Olympic or World Championships Champions or medallists were taken for anthropometric and spiroergometric analysis. The mean+/-standard deviation age, mass, height of the subjects under investigation was as follows: waterpolo players; 23.1+/-2.7 years, 83.9+/-7 kg, 184.5+/-3.6 cm and kayakers; 26+/-2.7 years, 87.4+/-6.3 kg, 185+/-9.1 cm, respectively. For the body composition assessment the Drinkwater-Ross [5] body mass fractionation technique was used. Taking the necessary body dimensions the IBP [14] suggestions were taken into consideration. Subjects were examined in the laboratory using Jaeger 6000 LE treadmill with modified Bruce treadmill testing protocol [1] and Jaeger mu-DATASPIR analyzer. The functional status of the cardiopulmonary system of the subjects were represented by the following spiroergometric parameters: heart rate (HR) beats/min., breathing frequency (BF) L/min., minute ventilation (MV) L/min., oxygen pulse (O2P) ml., and volume of oxygen uptake (VO2) L/min. Sampling rate frequency was 50 Hz and obtained records were averaged for every 30-second interval. The comparison of the anthropometric characteristics of G1 and G2 revealed significant differences in body mass related bone and body mass related fat percentages. The significant difference of relative body fat percentage between the two sample is natural since the representatives of the cyclic movement structural swimming - which is the major training exercise in waterpolo - have high values 12.1+/-1.6 [13], 14.1+/-2.9 [8] of relative fat percentage. In those cyclic sport-branches where athletes move their body and equipment mass under normal environmental circumstances the relative body fat percentage is always lower 8.9+/-2.67 [6], 10.6+/-3.8 [11]. The maximal spiroergometric parameters measured in both groups had similar average values. BF and O2P mean values were almost identical. MV and HR mean values were greater for G1 with 33.4 L, and 19.3 L/min. as compared to G2. The mean value of VO2 for G1 was 0.71 L/min. lower than the corresponding value of G2. However, these differences were not significant because of the high variability of the individual data around the means. The corresponding temporal - testing stages between 9 and 11.5 minutes - physiology performances nevertheless resulted in different physical performances. In the selected time interval significant difference was found between the VO2 results of G1 and G2 from 9.5 minute until the 11th minute. Differences were also significant (p<0.05) between the two groups in respective values of O2P (9,5 and 10.5-11min.), MV (10-11.5 min.) and BF (11-11.5 min.). The temporal development of the significant differences is mainly the consequence of that fact that subjects of G1 were in steady state while subjects of G2 were at vitae maxima. The significant differences among MV, O2P, VO2 and BF variables, which describe the physical performances in the selected interval, can be caused by the testing protocol as suggested by relevant literature.
