AB  - PURPOSE: Training zone identification forms part of the basis for exercise intensity distribution study and implementation in healthcare, prevention and rehabilitation/therapy. Although there may be different schools of thought on what type of distribution is "optimal" (polarized, pyramidal, threshold) most models are defined by having the major portion of training in a low intensity zone below the first ventilatory threshold (VT1). Recently, analysis of heart rate (HR) time series correlation properties, the short-term scaling exponent alpha1 of Detrended Fluctuation Analysis (DFA a1) has shown potential to delineate VT1 in recreational runners. The current study aims to extend the validity of this concept to a group of participants with cardiac disease. METHODS: Sixteen volunteers with stable coronary disease or heart failure performed an incremental cycling ramp to exhaustion before and after a 3 week training intervention. Oxygen intake (VO<sub>2</sub>) and HR at VT1 were obtained from a metabolic cart. A simultaneous ECG was processed by Kubios HRV Software for DFA a1 and HR. The heart rate variability threshold (HRVT) was defined as the VO<sub>2</sub> or HR where DFA a1 reached a value of .75. RESULTS: In pooled analysis of all ramp tests, mean VT1 was reached at 16.8±5.7 ml/kg/min with a HR of 91.3±12 bpm compared to HRVT which was reached at 18.0±7.7 ml/kg/min with a HR of 94.7±14 bpm. Strong linear relationships were seen between test modalities, with Pearson's r values of .95 (p<.001) for VO<sub>2</sub> and .86 (p<.001) for HR. Bland Altman assessment showed mean differences of 1.2 ml/kg/min (LOA 7.0 to -4.6, p=.03) for VO<sub>2</sub> and 3.4 bpm (LOA 17.8 to -11.0, p=.01) for HR. Mean VO<sub>2</sub>MAX (PRE 29.15±8.4, POST 30.73±10 ml/kg min, p=.07) and VT1 (PRE 16.61±5.5, POST 17.02±6.1 ml/kg/min, p=.6) did not change after training intervention. However, the correlation between PRE to POST change of VO<sub>2</sub> at VT1 with the change in VO<sub>2</sub> at HRVT was significant (r=.84, p<.001). CONCLUSION: There is a strong association of reaching a DFA a1 of .75 with the VT1 in a population with cardiac disease during an incremental ramp. The variable improvement in VT1 after training intervention closely followed that of the HRVT, confirming both the strong relationship between these parameters as well as offering a promising noninvasive tool for the purpose of exercise intensity distribution. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
