AB  - The article focuses on the effects of blood flow restricted (BFR) exercise on venous return, hormone release, and aerobic capacity training response. The study involved 13 participants who underwent BFR during semi-recumbent cycling, with tourniquets applied to their upper thighs. Results indicated that BFR significantly reduced venous return during exercise and recovery, while also increasing plasma renin and anti-diuretic hormone (ADH) concentrations. After two weeks of BFR training during walking, participants showed an improvement in absolute VO2max, suggesting that BFR may enhance aerobic capacity through its impact on blood volume-regulating hormones. Extracted from the article
