AB  - This study was conducted to investigate the effects of land-based aerobic exercise (LBE) and water-resistance aerobic exercise (WRE) on the physical fitness of primarily septuagenarian woman (mean age = 70 plus/minis 3.2 yrs). Seventy-six healthy sedentary women volunteered to partake in this study. They were randomly assigned to three groups; LBE, WRE and a control group. The two exercise groups were involved in 12 weeks, 3 times/week for 45 mins of mainly aerobic exercise. The education-control group was instructed in wellness lectures at least once per week. Aerobic capacity was tested at pre, mid and post sessions. Results indicated significant changes (p less than .05) by total group over time VO2max (1.22 to 1.35 1.min-1); (18.5 to 20.7 ml-kg-1 .min-1). However, no significant differences were evident between interventions. Other variables such as weight (66.3 to 66.5 kg); trunk flexion (30.5 to 29.9 cm); total grip strength (45.6 to 49.6 kg), and the sum of skinfolds demonstrated no change. There is a paucity of research available on elderly women. Results from this study indicated aerobic training adaptations are possible in septuagenarian women, however, statistical significance was not evident between the interventions over time. The probable cause for the lack of significance is discussed.
