AB  - PURPOSE: Rating of perceived exertion (RPE) can only reflect the overall feeling of fatigue. This study was to explore the correlation between specific muscle feeling(MF), RPE and blood lactic acid concentration (Bla) of female college students in the incremental load test of power bicycle. METHODS: A total of 27 female college students (mean age 24.3±3.22 years) without sports training experience participated in the incremental load experiment. The experiment was terminated according to American College of Sport Medicine standard with 50W power as starting power. Each stage lasts for 3 minutes, increasing 25W step by step at a constant speed of 60- 70 rpm. Test index: heart rate, Bla, RPE and score of muscle feeling scale, which is designed through preparatory experiment. Spss23.0 software was used for descriptive analysis of MF, RPE and Bla under different load intensity, and Pearson method was used to analyze the correlation of each index. RESULTS: MF, RPE and Bla are positively correlated with the load intensity. There are significant correlations between MF and RPE under the load of 50W, 75W, 100W and 125W, (r=0.852, 0.854, 0.879 and 0.897, respectively; p<0.01). There is no significant correlation between MF and Bla (p> 0.05). There is significant correlation between RPE and Bla under 150W exercise load (r=0.946, p < 0.01). CONCLUSIONS: For female college students, MF and RPE can be used to predict exercise intensity under low and moderate load, and specific muscle feeling can be used to estimate overall subjective fatigue, but the situation of high-intensity exercise still needs to be further explored. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
