AB  - To maintain high baroreflex sensitivity (BRS) is essential for the prevention of cardiovascular disease in middle-aged and older adults. Prior work demonstrated that masters endurance athletes have high BRS evaluated during spontaneous blood pressure (BP) fluctuations with seated resting condition and forced BP oscillations with repeated sit-stand maneuvers. However, it remains unclear this finding applies to general middle-aged and older population. PURPOSE: The purpose of this study was to examine the associations of cardiorespiratory fitness with BRS that was assessed during the seated resting condition and the repeated sit-stand maneuvers in middle-aged and older adults. METHODS: 168 middle-aged and older individuals were tested. BRS was evaluated during the two conditions 1) 5 minutes of the seated rest and 2) 5 minutes of the repeated sit-stand maneuvers at 0.05 Hz. In these conditions, beat-to-beat change of BP and R-R interval were measured, and transfer function analysis was conducted to calculate the cardiovagal BRS gain, phase, and coherence (input, systolic BP; output, R-R interval). Cardiorespiratory fitness was measured as oxygen consumption at the ventilation threshold (VO<sub>2VT</sub>) using cycle ergometer exercise test. Participants were classified as higher and lower cardiorespiratory fitness groups by a median split of VO<sub>2VT</sub> data. RESULTS: The BRS gain, phase, and coherence during the resting condition were significantly correlated with corresponding variables during the repeated sit-stand maneuvers (P < 0.001 for all). However, BRS gain and phase during the repeated sit-stand maneuvers were significantly lower and the coherence was significantly higher than those during the resting condition (P < 0.001 for all). A mixed-analysis of covariance (adjusted for age and sex) showed a significant interaction of cardiorespiratory fitness group and BRS gain assessment (F = 4.243, P = 0.041). Specifically, during repeated sit-stand maneuvers, high fitness group showed higher BRS gain than low fitness groups (P = 0.006) but BRS gain at rest was similar between both groups (P = 0.966). CONCLUSIONS: Higher cardiorespiratory fitness is associated with the higher BRS gain that was evaluated during forced BP oscillations using repeated sit-stand maneuvers in middle-aged and older adults. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
