TY  - ABST
AB  - A previous research study found that a single learning trial, on the seated bench press exercise, did not reveal a significant contribution toward increasing the subjects' self-selected resistance loads on any exercises that were studied. It remained unclear whether increasing the number of exercises in the learning trial would have significant influence on the subjects' physical and mental efforts for their self-selected loads. In the current study, the subjects were assigned two different types of learning trials (60% of their 1RM learning trial and RPE-AM, the “somewhat hard” learning trial) on eight typically performed resistance training exercises during the circuit weight training. All subjects experienced both learning trials in a repeated measures design. The order of the learning trials was randomized and counterbalanced. PURPOSE: To examine the effects of learning trials on young healthy untrained males' self-selected loads for eight commonly used exercises, during the circuit weight training. Self-selected loads after the subjects experienced the imposed 60% of 1RM (repetition maximum) learning trial and the subjects experienced the RPE (ratings of perceived exertion) learning trial were examined. METHODS: Twenty-one untrained healthy college-aged male subjects (mean age ± SD = 21.43 ± 2.29 years) were recruited from the University of Nebraska-Lincoln campus community for this study. Prior to the experimental sessions, the subjects participated in a familiarization session to learn the operation of eight selectorized machines arranged in a circuit fashion, at a cadence of 40 beats/minute, and in using an OMNI-RPE scale chart to rate their perceived exertion. The subjects' OMNI RPE-AM (active muscle) of 6 (somewhat hard) weights and the subjects' 60% of their 1RMs for each exercise were used for their separate learning trials. After each learning trial was completed, the subjects subsequently self-selected weights that they believed would improve their muscular fitness. RESULTS: A 2 (groups) X 5 (selected weights) MANOVA with repeated measures revealed that there were significant (Λ = 0.10, F = (32, 256.06) = 6.95, p ≤ 0.001, partial η<sup>2</sup> = 0.44) differences among pre-self-selected weights (PreSSweights), post-60% of 1RM learning trial self-selected weights (PostSSweightsA), and post-RPE 6 learning trial self-selected weights (PostSSweightsB). Follow-up univariate analyses revealed that the learning effects made significant (p < 0.001) contributions to all exercises of the selected weight scores. Pairwise comparisons for the selected weights were further analyzed to determine which variable was significantly different from others. CONCLUSIONS: All eight exercises' PostSSweightsA were significantly heavier than those of PreSSweights. In addition, all eight exercises' PostSSweightsA were significantly heavier than those of PostSSweightsB, except the rowing exercise. PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS: The current study indicated that if an untrained male undergoes a circuit weight training program for first time it is beneficial for him to experience imposed relatively higher intensity (i.e., 60% of 1RM) specific to the exercises that will be performed during his orientation or learning trial. This kinesthetic experience, regarding the intensity of the exercise during the learning trial, may transfer to a future session when he has to choose his own weights. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
