AB  - Collegiate swimmers practice many hours, including morning and evening training sessions, and compete at some meets without tapering. These training and competition methods may lead to under recovery and declines in performance. PURPOSE: To monitor the stress and recovery of swimmers throughout a collegiate season. METHODS: Twenty-one female Division I intercollegiate swimmers (mean age = 19.5 ± 1.2y, mean height = 168.6 ± 5.6cm, mean weight = 65.6 ± 7.7kg) participated in the study after reading and signing an approved informed consent form. The training load for each session was measured using rate of perceived exertion (RPE: 1 - 10 scale). Each individual's daily RPE score was multiplied by training duration to estimate training load (TL). The RESTQ-SPORT 76 (Cronbach's α = .85) was given monthly (T1, T2, T3, T4, T5 & T6). The data was then placed into the RESTQ-SPORT computer program to calculate mean scores for recovery-stress state, general stress, and general recovery scores. RESULTS: Training loads varied across the season (T1 = 2603.5 ± 652.79, T2 = 5888.9 ± 912.72, T3 = 4057.8 ± 960.17, T4 = 5930.7 ± 1031.48, T5 = 4573.7 ± 666.23, T6 = 3294.4 ± 459.09). For RESTQ scores a Repeated Measures ANOVA was run on each mean and significance was set at p<0.05. There was a significant time effect for scores over the season (p = 0.001) for RESTQ total. A simple contrast using the T1 RESTQ-Sport as a baseline (M = 9.24 ± 10.19) showed the RESTQ total was significantly less at T2 (M = 3.89 ± 10.23, p = 0.004), T4 (M = 2.02 ± 11.87, p = 0.022), and T5 (M = 3.9 ± 10.75, p = 0.038). There was no significance for global recovery. Global stress showed a significant time effect for scores over the season (p = 0.000). A simple contrast using T1 RESTQ-Sport as a baseline (M = 20.1 ± 5.50) showed that T2 (M = 24.00 ± 6.80 p<0.000), T3 (M = 22.9 ± 7.31, p = 0.048), T4 (M = 26.4 ± 7.69, p = 0.000), and T5 (M = 23.2 ± 7.70, p = 0.034) were significantly less than baseline. CONCLUSIONS: This study suggests that the RESTQ instrument is appropriate to monitor athletes across a season. RESULTS strongly suggest that as training load accumulates through a season the internal stress increases. This can lead to an increased risk of under recovery. PRACTICAL APPLICATIONS: Coaches can use RPE estimated training load and RESTQ scores to better identify which athletes need added recovery time. In addition, swimmers should be taught better strategies for recovery to better take advantage of training and improve performance. ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR
