AB  - This study compared the cardiorespiratory responses of water polo players performing the head-in-the-water (HI) and the head-out-of-the-water (HO) front crawl swimming techniques. These comparisons were made to ascertain whether water polo players demonstrate specific training responses as they employ the HO front crawl during games. Seven members of the 1985 Western Australian Men's Water Polo team performed two randomly assigned incremental workload tethered swimming tests to exhaustion, one using the HI and the other the HO technique, for the measurement of maximal oxygen uptake (VO2max) maximal heart rate (HRmax), maximal ventilation (VEmax), ventilatory threshold (VT) and respiratory compensation threshold (RCT). The initial break from linearity in VE and VE/VO2 with invariant VE/VCO2 was used to determine the VT. The second breakpoint in VE and VE/VO2 together with a consistent rise in VE/VCO2 was used to determine the onset of the RCT. A correlated t-test revealed that no significant differences existed between VO2max, VEmax when subjects performed the HI or HO tethered swimming test. Significantly higher HR (bpm; % HRmax) and VO2 (1.min-1; % VO2max) values were achieved at VT and RCT when subjects performed the HO tethered swimming test (p is less than 0.05). The results suggested that water polo players demonstrate a specific training response at submaximal exercise intensities when performing the HO front crawl swimming technique.
