AB  - Gender differences in substrate utilisation during exercise are still the matter of debate. On the basis of studies focusing mainly on submaximal endurance exercise it was shown that well-trained women metabolise substrates similarly to well-trained men, which leads to conclusion that training status rather than gender determines the substrate selection during muscular work. Only in few reports the metabolic responses to all-out heavy exercise had been examined with respect to sex. In order to study this subject in state of an intense exercise, a research was undertaken with participation of 10 male and 9 female young rowers (aged 16-19 yrs) performing 2-km all-out rowing on an ergometer. Blood pH, hemoglobin, hematocrit, plasma inorganic phosphate (PIPh) and ammonia levels were measured before and after exercise. Mean power output was significantly lower in female than in male rowers but that difference disappeared when power output was related to lean body mass (LBM). Mean exercise-induced change in blood lactate concentration, as well as post-exercise PIPh and ammonia levels were markedly lower in women than in men. Mean power output relative to total mass or LBM was significantly correlated with the post-exercise blood lactate concentration in men but not in women. Results indicate that rowers of both sexes attained mean power output per kg of LBM during that all-out simulated rowing, which required a higher engagement of anaerobic processes in men as compared to women.
