AB  - The concentration of beta-endorphin (B-EP) was measured in 6 trained and 6 untrained cyclists during three intensities of exercise to determine the time course changes of B-EP. B-EP was determined by radioimmunoassay with less than 5 percent cross reactivity with beta-lipotrophin. A counter-balanced design was used to avoid an order effect from exercise intensity. Resting B-EP values were similar across visits. There were no differences in resting B-EP values comparing the trained (4.61 plus/minus 0.25 pmol.1-1) to the untrained (4.03 plus/minus 0.23 pmol.1-1) group. Cycling at 60 percent VO2max did not increase B-EP in either group at any time measured. Cycling at 70 percent VO2max increased B-EP by 10 min in both groups. The rate and magnitude of increase of B-EP were similar for both groups. B-EP changes at 80 percent VO2max were significantly greater that at 70 percent VO2max and were identical for the two groups. Both groups demonstrated increases by 5 min and further increases at 30 min of exercise. These changes occurred despite the fact that lactate levels were lower in the trained group at both 70 and 80 percent VO2max intensities. It is concluded that the time course change for B-EP is similar for trained and untrained individuals working at the same relative intensity of exercise and does not seem to be related to plasma lactate concentrations.
