AB  - Although interval training is a method commonly used to develop key sport performance factors, there is no model available to help coaches finding the appropriate combination of its different elements for an optimal development of performance, and to ascertain the associated training load. Hence, the purpose of this study was to develop an empirical and graphical model that coaches could use to design appropriate interval training sessions at intensities commonly targeted for the development of aerobic endurance, maximal aerobic power (MAP) and anaerobic capacity. The model was developed from Gardner and Purdy's computerized running training tables, modified and modeled by us so that training intensity is expressed relatively to the MAP of the athlete. The graphical form of the model shows the link between the elements of an interval training session. The model has several practical applications. It provides a simple tool for comparing the relative level of difficulty or fatigue associated with various type of interval training sessions, thus facilitating the design of a long term training plan where the training load can follow a periodization pattern. It gives coaches a point of reference when they need to adjust interval training sessions, yet want the level of difficulty to remain controlled. The model also has a practical interest at times when it may be difficult to assess the intensity at which athletes train: performing the number of repetitions in the pattern derived from the model brings the athlete to spontaneously adopt an intensity close enough to the one that is assumed by the model, so that the desired physiological adaptations can be expected to occur. The model and its applications, in all sports where aerobic endurance, MAP and anaerobic capacity are key performance factors, have been taught in the National Coaches Certification Program (NCCP, program of the Coaches Association of Canada) for a decade. Coaches who use this model reported that it significantly improved the quality of the training programs they design and that athletes who follow these programs improve their performance to a greater extent and at a faster rate. In conclusion, the suggested model of interval training programming has practical applications in the coaching of athletes in several sports. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] NOTE: Unpublished paper. Paper submitted in consideration for the 2005 SIRC Research Award. Includes bibliographical references (p. 15-16). ABSTRACT FROM CONTRIBUTOR
