AB  - This study was to examine the characteristics of O2 uptake kinetics on 40 percent VO2max constant work rate and the relationship between O2 uptake kinetic and aerobic performance, and to analyse the O2 deficit and blood lactate, 27 male subjects (year 19.8 plus/minus 1.5) were tested, of them 9 were distance runners, 10 were sprinters, 8 were non-trained. VO2 was measured by Earger Eos Gas Metabolic Autoanalysisystem. The result indicated that VO2 was increased to fit the single exponential function. VO2 half-time, O2 deficit and the changes of blood lactate were different in three groups: distance runners were less than sprinters. Sprinters were less than the non-trained. We also found linear regression (p is less than 0.01) between VO2max and VO2 half-time (r = -0.732), VO2max and O2 deficit (r = -0.608), VO2 and blood lactate (r = -0.495). Therefore, it suggests that O2 uptake kinetics is related to training adaptation. On constant work rate below anabolic threshold, VO2 half-time responded to individual aerobic performance and can serve as an index to evaluate aerobic performance.
