AB  - Various possible techniques of estimating the exercise heart rate during cycle ergometry have been evaluated in 20 young men, using a detailed analysis of electrocardiograms following maximal and sub-maximal effort on a cycle ergometer. It is shown that all methods of treating heart rate recovery data tend to under-estimate the true exercise reading. However, the error becomes small and statistically insignificant if observations are restricted to the first 10 seconds post exercise and a high relative intensity of effort is involved. It is argued that under such circumstances, the tendency for a post-exercise decline of heart rate is offset by a brief anaerobic overshoot. If subjects are allowed a 5 second interval for locating the pulse, exercise heart rates are under-estimated by a further 3-4 percent, while attempts to improve accuracy by use of a 20 or 30 second post-exercise count increase the average error by a further 3 and 6 percent respectively. It is stressed that differences in the perfusion of active muscles and subsequent postural pooling preclude the extrapolation of the present results to other modes of exercise such as running and stepping.
