AB  - The aim of this study was to investigate the level of oxygen uptake, perceived exertion and blood lactate concentration during the execution of a pre-season alpine ski fitness program. Eight subjects were tested for maximum oxygen uptake and maximum heart rate during running on a motordriven treadmill. During the execution of the ski fitness program the heart rate was continuously recorded on all subjects by means of pulsometers (Sport Tester, PE3000, Polar Electro Oy) and stored for later computer analysis. The perceived exertion was rated by means of a fatigue rating scale and blood samples were taken from the finger tip for subsequent analysis of blood lactate concentration. The approximative linear relationship between heart rate and oxygen uptake was utilized to determine the relative oxygen uptake. The results showed a variation in oxygen uptake between 50 percent and 92 percent of maximal oxygen uptake (average about 70 percent of maximal oxygen uptake. The variations in perceived exertion and blood lactate concentration coincided with the variations in relative oxygen uptake. An approximation of aerobic energy production resulted in a total value of 2243 KJ (534 Kcal) per training session. The results showed that this type of off season ski fitness training loads the cardiorespiratory system to a large extent and can thereby be considered to have an increasing effect on the maximal oxygen uptake.
