AB  - The relationships between energy intake, hematological parameters, psychological indices, and markers of over-training were analyzed over the course of a ten-week cross country season. Present and former Division II NCAA male, cross-country runners (n=6) volunteered for the study. The rationale for the experimental design was twofold: 1. Numerous indices were monitored, due to the multi-factorial nature of the over-training syndrome; 2. The over-training syndrome requires weeks to develop, implying the necessity for a long-term experimental design. Blood was drawn at the beginning (B), middle (M), and end (E) of the season, with dietary logs being kept during weeks B, M, and E. Training logs were recorded weekly. Repeated measures ANOVA revealed no significant differences in morning heart rate (MHR), sleep rating, dizziness (DZ), physical symptoms (PS), physical distress (PD), POMS indices, and blood parameters (p>0.05) across time points. Muscle stiffness (MS) decreased significantly from M to E (p=0.015). Training time and distance decreased by 41% and 38%, respectively, from B to E. Relative carbohydrate (p=0.025) and caloric intake (p=0.024) decreased significantly from B to E. Consistently high correlations between MHR, DZ, MS, PS, and PD were found from weeks 3 to 9. Training mode and relative carbohydrate intake were inversely related to markers of over-training at M. Hematological parameters did not vary consistently across and within time points. The findings of this study were: 1. Training mode appears to moderate training load; 2. Carbohydrate intake appears to mediate training load; 3. Subjective indices were successful at monitoring training status; 4. Testosterone, cortisol, and the FTCR were not consistent markers of training status.
